window.LESSONS=[{"id":"lecture-01","lecture":1,"title":"From data to a DBMS","short":"Database, abstraction & course map","group":"01 · Foundations","time":25,"lead":"Start with a design question: what could go wrong if every feature in an application managed its own data?","goals":["Distinguish a database, a DBMS, a schema, and an instance","Explain the three levels of abstraction through an example","Identify the system responsibilities behind a query"],"sections":[{"id":"l1-1","title":"A guide to the slides","html":"<p>A database is an organised collection of data, such as a library’s books and loans. A DBMS (Database Management System) is the software that manages how that data is stored, read, and updated. This lecture introduces the reasons for using a DBMS, the weaknesses of separate files, the view/logical/physical levels, and data independence: the ability to change one level while limiting the effect on the level above. The remaining slides cover the course roadmap, assessment, and course policies.</p><p>Read PDF pages 10–19 first; pages 21–35 provide context for the Spring 2026 course. Administrative dates belong to that historical slide edition and are not current submission deadlines.</p>"},{"id":"l1-2","title":"Original design exercise: a neighbourhood library","html":"<p><strong>An original scenario.</strong> A library keeps books.csv and loans.csv. Initially, a book has identifier B17 and its loan also refers to B17. Mai changes the identifier in the books file to B017, but the program stops before updating the loans file: the loan still refers to B17, an identifier that no longer exists. Someone else then overwrites loans.csv with an older copy, making a new loan disappear.</p><ol><li>Draw the data before and after each write. Identify exactly which relationship is lost.</li><li>Write the business rules: every loan must refer to an existing book; assuming each book identifier represents one physical copy, each copy may have only one open loan.</li><li>Distinguish a modelling error, an update-procedure error, and an error caused by two people writing concurrently.</li></ol><p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The rule that a book identifier must exist is a data constraint. Changing a book identifier and updating its related loans should form one transaction, a unit of work containing several operations: either both changes finish, or no partial update remains. Overwriting the current file with an older copy is an access-coordination problem. Later lectures introduce the specific transaction mechanisms.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Changing the file format does not fix update errors.</b> Both CSV and JSON can contain broken references or suffer overwritten changes. The system needs constraint checking, all-or-nothing updates, and coordinated writes.</p></aside>"},{"id":"l1-3","title":"Three views of the same system","html":"<p><strong>An original example.</strong> A librarian sees borrowers’ names and due dates; visitors searching the catalogue see only book titles and availability. This is the view level: each user group sees the data relevant to it. At the logical level, we describe Book(book_id, title) and Loan(book_id, borrower, due). At the physical level, we decide how records and indexes, the structures that help locate data, are arranged.</p><p>Book(book_id, title) is a schema: it describes the table’s columns and structure. The current rows, such as (B017, “Databases”), form an instance: the data at a particular moment. Adding a book changes the instance; adding a publication-year column changes the schema.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A schema describes structure; an instance is the current data.</b> Distinguishing these changes helps you decide whether an application needs another data row or a new structure for representing its data.</p></aside><p>Ask yourself: should changing the page size, the unit managed during reads and writes, from 4 KiB to 8 KiB force the catalogue application to rewrite its queries? Physical data independence helps keep logical queries stable when storage details change.</p><details><summary>Check yourself: at which level do we add a borrower’s email address?</summary><p>Adding the email field changes the logical description of the data. Its storage offset is a physical detail. Deciding who may see it belongs to view and access-control design.</p></details>"},{"id":"l1-4","title":"A roadmap for independent practice","html":"<p>The exercises on this website use new examples and are not official assessed submissions: ask a data question → write a relational expression → count page I/Os → design buffers and indexes → choose an execution plan → preserve correct results under concurrency and failures.</p><p>Before Lecture 2, write a plain-language question about the library and state its expected answer for three data rows. You do not need an algorithm yet. That expected answer will help you check the execution methods introduced later.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Changing a record’s byte position primarily belongs to which level?","options":["View","Logical","Physical"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A byte position is a physical representation detail; the schema describes the meaning and structure of the data."},"source":{"summary":"Course Introduction: motivation, database/DBMS, flat-file weaknesses, abstraction, data models; course organisation, assessment and academic integrity.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pp. 10–19 · databases, DBMSs, and abstraction; no printed slide numbers"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pp. 21–35 · roadmap and information for the Spring 2026 course"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–36","pdfPageCount":36,"topics":["Motivation","Flat files","DBMS","Abstraction","Data independence","Data models/schema","Course organisation","Assessment","Academic integrity"],"limitations":["Intro has no printed slide numbers; citations use physical PDF page numbers.","Administrative dates apply to Spring 2026 only."],"week":1,"officialTitle":"Course Introduction","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-1","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"E SSENTIAL Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #1: U TILITY"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #2: C ENTRALITY"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"How much data is"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"S CALE OF S CIEN TIFIC D ATA"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #2: C ENTRALITY"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #3: T HE C ORE OF C OMPUTING"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"E SSENTIAL Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT IS A D ATABASE ?"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT IS A DBMS?"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY USING A DBMS?"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE S TRAWMAN"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE S TRAWMAN"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE : D RAWBACKS"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE : D RAWBACKS (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE : D RAWBACKS (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"L EVELS OF A BSTRACTIONS"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"D ATA M ODELS"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"R ANKING OF DBMS T ECHNOLOGIES 2026"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT I S T HIS C OURSE A BOUT ?"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT I S T HIS C OURSE A BOUT ?"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"L EARNING O UTCOMES"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"C OURSE P REREQUISITES"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"E SSENTIAL Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HO I S R UNNING T HIS C OURSE ?"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"H OW W ILL T HIS C OURSE W ORK ?"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"L ECTURE O VERVIEW"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"A SSESSMENT S TRUCTURE"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"P ROGRAMMING A SSIGNMENT"},{"pdfPage":31,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"T EXTBOOKS"},{"pdfPage":32,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"R ECENT C OURSE C HANGES"},{"pdfPage":33,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"S TUDENT F EEDBACK FROM P REVIOUS Y EARS"},{"pdfPage":34,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"P LAGIARISM P OLICY"},{"pdfPage":35,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"S TAYING IN T OUCH"},{"pdfPage":36,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"A CKNOWLEDGEMENT"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":9},"lab":null},{"id":"lecture-02","lecture":2,"title":"SQL: understanding the result","short":"Bags, grouping, joins & subqueries","group":"01 · Foundations","time":25,"lead":"Learn SQL by working out the result table before running the statement.","goals":["Work through WHERE, GROUP BY, and HAVING step by step","Distinguish inner and outer joins and calculate their result sizes","Use IN and EXISTS with small datasets"],"sections":[{"id":"l2-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>SQL describes the desired result, such as “total sales by city”. The optimiser chooses an execution plan to compute that result. SQL normally uses bag semantics: duplicate rows remain unless duplicate elimination is requested with DISTINCT.</p><p>This lecture covers ORDER BY/LIMIT, aggregates such as SUM, GROUP BY/HAVING, joins that combine data, and subqueries nested within another query. To calculate results yourself, follow the logical order FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → DISTINCT → ORDER BY → LIMIT. This order explains the statement’s meaning; it does not require the DBMS to execute the physical operations in that sequence.</p>"},{"id":"l2-2","title":"An original dataset and query","html":"<p>Let Sale(id, city, amount) contain (1, Hanoi, 40), (2, Hanoi, 70), (3, Hue, 30), (4, Hue, 90), (5, Hue, 10). Each row represents a sale; id identifies it, city names the city, and amount is measured in the same monetary unit. Find cities whose total exceeds 100, counting only sales of at least 30.</p><pre><code>SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS total\nFROM Sale\nWHERE amount &gt;= 30\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 100\nORDER BY total DESC, city ASC;</code></pre><ol><li>FROM provides five sales. WHERE examines individual rows and removes id=5 because 10 &lt; 30, leaving ids 1, 2, 3, 4.</li><li>GROUP BY groups the remaining rows by city: Hanoi has amounts 40, 70; Hue has 30, 90.</li><li>SUM adds the values within each group: Hanoi has 40+70=110; Hue has 30+90=120. HAVING retains both because each total is &gt; 100.</li><li>SELECT returns city and names the sum total. ORDER BY sorts total in descending order, using ascending city order to break ties. The result is (Hue,120), (Hanoi,110).</li></ol><p>Now change WHERE to amount &gt; 50: only id=2 and id=4 remain, so the totals are 70 and 90. HAVING rejects both groups and the final result is empty.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>WHERE filters rows before aggregation; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.</b> Changing WHERE changes which numbers enter SUM. Changing HAVING only determines whether an already-computed group appears.</p></aside>"},{"id":"l2-3","title":"Joins and subqueries: an experiment with answers","html":"<p><strong>A new dataset.</strong> Member(mid,name) = {(1,An),(2,Binh),(3,Chi)}; Borrow(mid,book) = {(1,A),(1,B),(2,C)}. The member identifier mid connects the tables; M and B below are table aliases.</p><pre><code>SELECT M.name, B.book\nFROM Member M LEFT JOIN Borrow B ON M.mid=B.mid\nORDER BY M.mid, B.book;</code></pre><p>An matches two Borrow rows with mid=1, producing (An,A) and (An,B). Binh matches one row, producing (Binh,C). Chi matches none, but LEFT JOIN preserves this member and fills the Borrow side with NULL. The result is (An,A), (An,B), (Binh,C), (Chi,NULL). An inner join returns only the first three rows.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>One left-hand row can produce several result rows.</b> A join produces one row for every matching pair. LEFT JOIN adds a NULL-padded row only when no match exists; it does not limit each member to one row.</p></aside><pre><code>SELECT M.name FROM Member M\nWHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Borrow B WHERE B.mid=M.mid)\nORDER BY M.mid;</code></pre><p>EXISTS asks only whether at least one loan exists. An and Binh have loans; Chi does not, so the result is An, Binh. An’s two loans still make the condition true just once for An’s Member row. With this NULL-free dataset, replacing the condition with M.mid IN (SELECT B.mid FROM Borrow B) gives the same result: IN tests whether the identifier appears in the list 1,1,2.</p><p>These queries serve different purposes: the join retrieves individual loans, while EXISTS retrieves members who have loans. Do not use one query’s row count as the answer for the other.</p>"},{"id":"l2-4","title":"Independent practice and scope","html":"<p>On Sale, SELECT city returns 5 rows; SELECT DISTINCT city returns 2 values. Write a query that returns the highest-value sale, adding id to ORDER BY to resolve ties when several rows have the same amount.</p><details><summary>A suggested answer</summary><pre><code>SELECT id, city, amount FROM Sale\nORDER BY amount DESC, id ASC LIMIT 1;</code></pre><p>The result is (4,Hue,90).</p></details><p>ALL/ANY are also introduced on PDF page 28. For the set {30,90}, 50 &gt; ANY is true and 50 &gt; ALL is false. This example contains no NULL values. NULL’s three-valued logic, TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN, needs supplementary reading; it cannot be inferred from an outer-join diagram.</p><p>Read these points together: slide 14 simplifies where aggregates can occur, but slide 18 shows an aggregate in HAVING. A correlated subquery can be understood row by row, although the optimiser may transform its execution.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"In Sale, what is Hue’s total after WHERE amount >= 30?","options":["130","120","90"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The row with amount=10 is filtered out, leaving 30+90=120. WHERE filters individual rows before GROUP BY computes SUM, so the sale of 10 does not contribute."},"source":{"summary":"SQL terminology and history; DDL/DML/DCL; SELECT, sorting, limits, aggregates, grouping, joins, IN/EXISTS, ALL/ANY.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=6","label":"PDF pp. 6–12 / printed slides 7–13 · tables and SELECT"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pp. 13–19 / slides 14–20 · aggregates and evaluation"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pp. 20–28 / slides 21–29 · joins and nested queries"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–29","pdfPageCount":29,"topics":["SQL history","DDL/DML/DCL","Schema/instance","Bags","SELECT/DISTINCT","ORDER BY/LIMIT","Aggregates","GROUP BY/HAVING","Joins","Outer joins","IN/EXISTS","ALL/ANY"],"limitations":["Three-valued NULL logic is not taught here.","Conceptual SQL order is not physical execution order."],"week":1,"officialTitle":"SQL","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-1","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"SQL H ISTORY"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"SQL’ S P ERSISTENCE"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"SQL P ROS AND C ONS"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"R ELATIONAL T ERMINOLOGY"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"R ELATIONAL TABLES"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"SQL L ANGUAGE"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"E XAMPLE D ATABASE"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"B ASIC S INGLE -TABLE Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"O RDER B Y                                 ORDER BY <column*> [ASC|DESC]"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"L IMIT                                                   LIMIT <count> [offset]"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"A GGREGATES"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"G ROUP B Y"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"G ROUP B Y"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"F ILTER G ROUPS"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"H AVING"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"C ONCEPTUAL SQL E VALUATION"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"M ULTIPLE -TABLE Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"J OIN Q UERY"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"J OIN Q UERY – A NOTHER S YNTAX"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"J OIN VARIANTS"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"L EFT O UTER J OIN"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"R IGHT O UTER J OIN"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"F ULL O UTER J OIN"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"N ESTED Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"N ESTED Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"M ORE ON S ET-C OMPARISON O PERATORS"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"sql"},{"id":"lecture-03","lecture":3,"title":"Relational algebra: from questions to plans","short":"Selection, projection & composition","group":"01 · Foundations","time":25,"lead":"A compact expression can describe an entire query, but every operator needs a clear schema and semantics.","goals":["Evaluate relational operators on small datasets","Distinguish set projection from SQL bag projection","Turn a join question into an expression you can check"],"sections":[{"id":"l3-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>To answer “which products cost more than 25?”, we can combine a products table with a prices table, retain qualifying rows, and select the columns we need. Relational algebra (RA) gives names to operations like these. Each operator takes relations, table-shaped collections, and returns a relation, so one step’s result can become the next step’s input.</p><p>The lecture connects calculus, algebra, and query plans. The basic operations are selection, which retains rows; projection, which retains columns; rename; union; difference; and product, which combines every pair of rows. Intersection and joins can be built from them. Pure RA uses set semantics and does not retain duplicates. SQL normally uses bag semantics and can retain duplicates. The final slides also list aggregation, duplicate elimination, assignment, sorting, and division.</p>"},{"id":"l3-2","title":"An original example: rows, columns, and joins","html":"<p>The example has two tables. R(pid,category) stores product identifiers and categories; S(pid,price) stores product identifiers and prices:</p><pre><code>R(pid, category)\n(11, tea)\n(12, tea)\n(13, cake)\n\nS(pid, price)\n(11, 20)\n(13, 35)\n(14, 50)</code></pre><p><strong>Keep the category column.</strong> Read R’s three rows and remove pid. The intermediate values are tea, tea, cake. The first two rows had different identifiers, but removing those identifiers makes both rows tea. Because a pure RA result is a set, retain one copy of each row: the final result is {tea,cake}.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Projection in pure RA removes duplicate rows after selecting columns.</b> R has no fully identical rows, but removing pid can make two rows identical. SQL SELECT category retains tea, tea, cake; SELECT DISTINCT category removes duplicates.</p></aside><p>Only now introduce the notation: π category(R) = {tea,cake}. Read this as “project the category column from R”. The symbol π denotes projection, category specifies the column, and R is the input. Compare <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 7–9</a> on set semantics, selection, and projection.</p><ul><li><strong>Keep qualifying rows.</strong> Examine each R row’s category and retain only tea: {(11,tea),(12,tea)}. Write σ category=tea(R); σ denotes selection, and its condition determines which rows survive.</li><li><strong>Combine every pair.</strong> Each R row combines with all three S rows, producing 3×3=9 tuples, including pairs whose identifiers do not match. Write R × S.</li><li><strong>Keep only pairs with the same pid.</strong> Among the nine pairs, only identifiers 11 and 13 match. The theta join R ⋈ R.pid=S.pid S returns columns (R.pid,category,S.pid,price) with rows (11,tea,11,20), (13,cake,13,35).</li><li><strong>Return the required columns.</strong> Remove both pid columns from the join result to obtain {(tea,20),(cake,35)}. Write π category,price(R ⋈ R.pid=S.pid S).</li></ul><p>Distinguish R.pid from S.pid in a theta join, a join with an explicit condition. A natural join automatically compares every shared column name, so adding a same-named column to both tables can change its join condition.</p>"},{"id":"l3-3","title":"Two plans, one result: a data-based proof","html":"<p>We want the categories of products with price &gt; 25. <strong>Plan A:</strong> join R and S first, obtaining product 11 at price 20 and product 13 at price 35; the price filter keeps product 13. <strong>Plan B:</strong> filter S first, obtaining (13,35) and (14,50); joining with R keeps only identifier 13 because R has no identifier 14. Both plans then project category and return {cake}.</p><p>Expression A: π category(σ price&gt;25(R ⋈ R.pid=S.pid S)). Expression B: π category(R ⋈ R.pid=S.pid σ price&gt;25(S)). Read from the innermost operation outwards: input → join/filter → select columns. A produces two join rows and filters them down to one; B first filters S down to two rows and then produces one join row.</p><p><strong>Additional reasoning.</strong> The predicate, or filter condition, uses only S.price. Every S row with price ≤ 25 will be rejected regardless of the R row it joins with, so the condition can be checked early.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Push a condition only into an input containing every column it needs.</b> If the condition needs both R and S, S alone lacks enough information to evaluate it. This argument applies to the example’s inner join; it must not be transferred unchanged to an outer join.</p></aside><p>This exercise practises proving that two plans are equivalent. Detailed optimisation rules appear in later lectures.</p>"},{"id":"l3-4","title":"Set exercises and self-check","html":"<p>Let A(x)={1,2,4} and B(x)={2,3}. Compute their union, difference, and intersection before revealing the answer.</p><details><summary>Answer</summary><p>A∪B={1,2,3,4}; A−B={1,4}; A∩B={2}; A−(A−B)={2}. If two schemas have incompatible column counts or types, adjust the schemas before applying these set operations.</p></details><p>A deeper question: must π category(R) contain fewer rows than R? No. If all three categories are different, the row count stays unchanged. EXCEPT ALL is only named in the deck; a formula for each row’s multiplicity is not developed here.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"If R has 3 rows and S has 4 rows, how many rows does R × S contain?","options":["7","12","At most 4"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each R row combines with every S row: 3×4=12. The Cartesian product has not yet applied a join condition; a later selection may reduce the number of pairs."},"source":{"summary":"Query compilation and Codd’s theorem; compositional relational algebra, set semantics, core operators, joins and additional operators.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–7 / slides 2–7 · plans, calculus, and semantics"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=8","label":"PDF pp. 8–18 / slides 8–18 · operators"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=19","label":"PDF pp. 19–20 / slides 19–20 · additional operators"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–20","pdfPageCount":20,"topics":["Query execution","Calculus/RA","Codd theorem","Closure/types","Sets/bags","Selection","Projection","Union/difference","Product/rename","Intersection","Theta/equi/natural join","Extra operators"],"limitations":["Division is listed, not developed.","Do not reproduce slide 11 erroneous S−R table."],"week":1,"officialTitle":"Relational Algebra","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-1","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"Q UERY E XECUTION O VERVIEW"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"Q UERY E XECUTION O VERVIEW"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"R ELATIONAL Q UERY L ANGUAGES"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"C ODD ’ S T HEOREM"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"R ELATIONAL A LGEBRA"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"R ELATIONAL A LGEBRA AND S ETS"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"S ELECTION"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"P ROJECTION"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"U NION                 R     S                R∪S"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"S ET D IFFERENCE                        R     S       R –S       S"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"C ROSS P RODUCT"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"R ENAMING (⍴ = “rho”)"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"C OMPOUND O PERATOR : I NTERSECTION"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"C OMPOUND O PERATOR : J OIN"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"T HETA J OIN E XAMPLE"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"T HETA J OIN E XAMPLE 2"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"N ATURAL J OIN"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"E XTRA O PERATORS"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":5},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-04","lecture":4,"title":"Hardware and the disk-space manager","short":"Pages, locality & I/O cost","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"An algorithm with little computation can still be slow if it forces the device to perform too many scattered I/Os.","goals":["Trace the path from SQL down to pages","Calculate read costs using an explicitly stated model","Distinguish bytes, pages, and records"],"sections":[{"id":"l4-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>A DBMS is organised into planning, operator execution, files/indexes, buffering, and disk-space management. Concurrency control and recovery span several layers. We compare hardware by latency, capacity, and durability. An HDD read can include seek time, moving the head to the required track; rotational latency, waiting for the required position to rotate under the head; and transfer time, moving the data. SSDs have no mechanical head and have different write characteristics. The disk-space manager handles page identifiers, allocation, and reads/writes.</p>"},{"id":"l4-2","title":"An original I/O exercise, not a benchmark","html":"<p>Consider an illustrative HDD model. To read a page at a new location, the head moves to the correct track (seek time = 4 ms), waits for the required disk region to rotate into position (rotational latency = 3 ms), and transfers the page (transfer time = 0.1 ms). A random page read therefore takes 4+3+0.1=7.1 ms.</p><p>Perform 200 random page reads one after another. Assume no cache hits, read-ahead, or parallel I/O; every request targets a new location and pays all three costs. Seeking and waiting for rotation occur 200 times, not just at the beginning. Total time is 200×(4+3+0.1)=1420 ms, or 1.42 seconds.</p><p>If the 200 pages are contiguous on disk and read as one continuous sequential scan, the head locates the first page and then continues transferring subsequent pages. In this simplified model, charge seek and rotational latency only once; ignore track changes, pauses, and all other overheads. Positioning costs 4+3=7 ms, while transfer costs 200×0.1=20 ms. Total time is 4+3+200×0.1=27 ms, so the random/sequential ratio is 1420/27 ≈ 52.6.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Random reads repeat positioning costs; a continuous run shares the initial cost.</b> This explains the difference between 1420 ms and 27 ms in the exercise. The numbers depend on the stated assumptions and are not a universal HDD or SSD speedup.</p></aside><p>Replace 200 with 2: random=14.2 ms and sequential=7.2 ms. Reading more consecutive pages spreads the initial seek and rotation costs across more pages.</p>"},{"id":"l4-3","title":"An original page-ID mapping example","html":"<p>Assume a file uses 4096-byte pages, has no file header, and numbers pages from 0. Before page 7 are pages 0 through 6, seven pages in total. Page 7’s offset, measured in bytes from the start of the file, is 7×4096=28672 bytes. A record begins at offset 120 within that page, so its file address is 28672+120=28792.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Identify each offset’s origin before adding offsets.</b> The value 28672 is measured from the start of the file; 120 is measured from the start of the page. Confusing those origins points to the wrong record even when the arithmetic is correct.</p></aside><p>Higher layers should request page 7 rather than hard-code a file path and byte offset. If the DBMS moves that page to another file, a mapping table can preserve the stable page-ID interface.</p><details><summary>Exercise: where does page 12 begin?</summary><p>Offset=12×4096=49152 bytes under the same assumptions. A file header of 512 bytes would require adding 512, so always state the addressing model.</p></details>"},{"id":"l4-4","title":"Interpreting hardware figures carefully","html":"<p>The PDF’s prices, capacities, and latencies are teaching examples for particular configurations and points in time. In a model-based exam question, use the supplied numbers. To assess a real system, measure its workload, cache behaviour, parallelism, and specific device.</p><p>Check yourself: is “how many I/Os are needed to read 1,000 records?” sufficiently specified? No. You need to know how many pages contain the records, whether those pages are already in RAM, and whether they are physically close together. These questions prepare you for buffering, page layout, and indexes.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Under the exercise’s model, how long does reading 200 contiguous pages take?","options":["1420 ms","27 ms","20 ms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Seek time plus rotational latency is 7 ms. Transferring 200 pages takes 20 ms, giving a total of 27 ms under the stated assumptions."},"source":{"summary":"Layered DBMS architecture; storage hierarchy; HDD/SSD; random versus sequential access; page abstraction and disk-space allocation/mapping.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–14 / slides 2–14 · architecture and the memory hierarchy"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=15","label":"PDF pp. 15–23 / slides 15–24 · devices and I/O"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=24","label":"PDF pp. 24–29 / slides 25–31 · the disk-space manager"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–29","pdfPageCount":29,"topics":["DBMS layers","Concurrency/recovery overview","Volatility","Hierarchy","HDD anatomy","Seek/rotation/transfer","Sequential locality","SSD/wear levelling","Pages","Allocation","Page-ID mapping"],"limitations":["Hardware figures are dated examples, not current purchasing advice."],"week":2,"officialTitle":"Hardware & Disk Space Management","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-2","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"DBMS: B IG P ICTURE"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"DBMS: Q UERY P LANNING"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"DBMS: O PERATOR E XECUTION"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"DBMS: F ILES & I NDEX M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"DBMS: B UFFER M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"DBMS: D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"A RCHITECTURE OF A DBMS"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"DBMS: C ONCURRENCY & R ECOVERY"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"D ISK -O RIENTED A RCHITECTURE"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"W HY N OT S TORE A LL IN M AIN M EMORY ?"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"S TORAGE H IERARCHY"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"S TORAGE H IERARCHY                           Latency   Capacity"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"A NATOMY OF A D ISK"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"A CCESSING A D ISK PAGE"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"Seagate Cheetah 15K.7                                           17"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"S EQUENTIAL VS . R ANDOM A CCESS"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"A RRANGING B LOCKS ON D ISK"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"S OLID S TATE D RIVES"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"S OLID S TATE D RIVES"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"SSD VS . HDD"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"B OTTOM L INE"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"D ATABASE S TORAGE"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"S YSTEM D ESIGN G OALS"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT : PAGE R EQUESTS"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT : I MPLEMENTATION"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":31,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"storage"},{"id":"lecture-05","lecture":5,"title":"The buffer pool: pin, dirty, LRU, and CLOCK","short":"Cache state & replacement traces","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"Trace the state: which pages may be replaced, which must be written back, and why?","goals":["Update pin counts and dirty flags correctly","Simulate LRU and CLOCK when some pages are pinned","Explain sequential flooding and the limits of replacement policies"],"sections":[{"id":"l5-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>A query needs page P. If P is already in RAM, the DBMS can use it immediately: this is a buffer hit. Otherwise, the DBMS must load P: this is a buffer miss. The buffer pool is the RAM area holding pages; each frame is a space that holds one page.</p><p>Pinning marks a page as in use; the pin count records outstanding uses that have not yet been released. Only a frame with pin count 0 may be selected for replacement. A dirty page has been modified in RAM but not yet written back to disk. Its modified contents must be written back before eviction removes the page from its frame. The lecture covers LRU based on the most recent unpin, CLOCK, MRU, sequential flooding, LRU-K, priority hints, multiple pools, prefetching, and differences from the OS cache.</p>"},{"id":"l5-2","title":"An original trace: pinning and dirty state","html":"<p>Initially P has pin=0 and dirty=false. Two users each call pin(P), increasing the counter by one each time: pin=2. The first user modifies the page and calls unpin(P,true): pin falls to 1 and dirty=true. The second user only reads and calls unpin(P,false): pin falls to 0, but dirty remains true.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Pin count and the dirty flag answer different questions.</b> Pin=0 means nobody still holds the page; dirty=true means there are changes not yet written to disk. Unpinning with false says only that this user made no modification. It does not erase an earlier user’s dirty state.</p></aside><p>P is now eligible for replacement, but is not necessarily evicted immediately. If a cache miss selects P as its victim, the cost may include one write of P and one read of the incoming page. A pin count also does not by itself prevent conflicting writes; coordinating them requires concurrency control.</p><details><summary>Check yourself: what if every frame is still pinned?</summary><p>No frame is eligible for eviction. An implementation must wait, report that no frame is available, or use another management mechanism. It cannot ignore pin counts and overwrite an in-use page. This is an additional implementation inference from the invariant, a condition that must always hold.</p></details>"},{"id":"l5-3","title":"An original LRU trace: eight requests, three frames","html":"<p>Assume each request pins its page and immediately unpins it; all three frames start empty and every page is clean. The request trace is A,B,C,A,D,B,A,C. LRU selects the eligible page whose last unpin is furthest in the past. Under this assumption, that is also the least recently used page.</p><ol><li>A,B,C: 3 misses; oldest→newest order is A,B,C.</li><li>A: hit; B,C,A.</li><li>D: miss, evict B; C,A,D.</li><li>B: miss, evict C; A,D,B.</li><li>A: hit; D,B,A.</li><li>C: miss, evict D; B,A,C.</li></ol><p>The result is 6 misses, 2 hits, and 0 write-backs. The first three misses fill empty frames; the next three replace B, C, and then D. If D is dirty when evicted, the miss count stays the same but one write is added.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Count cache misses and write-backs separately.</b> A miss means the requested page is absent from the pool. In this model, an extra write occurs only when a dirty page must be replaced; six misses do not mean six writes.</p></aside><p>With A,B,C,D repeating through three LRU frames, every request misses: each cycle needs four pages, but only three fit, and the page needed next has already been evicted. This is sequential flooding. “Recently used” does not guarantee “needed again soon”.</p>"},{"id":"l5-4","title":"An original CLOCK trace and comparison exercise","html":"<p>CLOCK moves a hand around the frames. The reference bit, ref, records recent use. When CLOCK encounters an unpinned page with ref=1, it clears the bit and gives the page another chance. Consider the ring F0,F1,F2 with the hand at F0. The frames contain A(pin=1,ref=1), B(pin=0,ref=1), and C(pin=0,ref=0), respectively. We need to load D.</p><ol><li>Skip A because it is pinned. Needing space does not make A eligible.</li><li>At B, set ref to 0 and continue.</li><li>At C, ref=0 and the page is unpinned, so select C.</li><li>Load D into F2, set pin=1 and ref=1, and advance the hand to F0.</li></ol><p>Practise by changing C.ref to 1. The hand skips A, clears B.ref and C.ref, then returns and skips A again. If no intervening access occurs, B is the first page encountered again with ref=0. Compare this with MRU, which replaces the most recently used page, on a repeated scan. Explain why no policy is optimal for every workload.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"P has dirty=true. What is dirty after unpin(P,false)?","options":["false","true","It depends on the pin count"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The new dirty flag is the old dirty flag OR the supplied dirty argument. true OR false remains true."},"source":{"summary":"Buffer metadata and pin/unpin; LRU/CLOCK/MRU; sequential flooding; LRU-K; priority hints, partitioned pools, prefetch and OS interaction.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pp. 3–11 / slides 3–11 · buffer state"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pp. 13–21 / slides 14–22 · LRU/CLOCK"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=22","label":"PDF pp. 22–35 / slides 26–39 · scans, MRU, and practical considerations"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–35","pdfPageCount":35,"topics":["Frames/page table","Pin/unpin","Dirty writeback","LRU","CLOCK","Sequential flooding","MRU","LRU-K","Priority hints","Partitioned pools","Prefetch","OS buffering"],"limitations":["Practical DBMS claims in slides are course context, not a current-version implementation guarantee."],"week":2,"officialTitle":"Buffer Management","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-2","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT : PAGE R EQUEST"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT : PAGE R ELEASE"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"O PEN Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGER S TATE"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"P ROPER P IN /U NPIN N ESTING"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"P IN I MPLEMENTATION"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"U NPIN I MPLEMENTATION"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"A DVANCED Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"B UFFER R EPLACEMENT P OLICIES"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"L EAST R ECENTLY U SED (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"L EAST R ECENTLY U SED (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"C LOCK R EPLACEMENT P OLICY                                             p1"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY S TATE : E XPLICIT & I LLUSTRATED"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"R EPLACEMENT P OLICIES C AN F AIL"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":31,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":32,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (MRU)"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":33,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (MRU)"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":34,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (MRU)"},{"pdfPage":31,"printedSlide":35,"heading":"B EST R EPLACEMENT P OLICY ?"},{"pdfPage":32,"printedSlide":36,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT IN P RACTICE"},{"pdfPage":33,"printedSlide":37,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT IN P RACTICE"},{"pdfPage":34,"printedSlide":38,"heading":"W HY N OT U SE THE OS?"},{"pdfPage":35,"printedSlide":39,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":9},"lab":"buffer"},{"id":"lecture-06","lecture":6,"title":"Files, pages, and records","short":"Heap files, slots & byte layout","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"From a tuple with named columns to a sequence of bytes: which layer keeps its address stable?","goals":["Calculate offsets and capacity under explicit assumptions","Explain packed pages, bitmaps, free lists, and slotted pages","Distinguish a slot directory from a field-offset directory"],"sections":[{"id":"l6-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>A database file consists of pages, and each page contains records. A record represents one row of data, while a page is the unit used to manage I/O in this model. A heap file does not require records to follow search-key order. Its pages may be managed through a linked list or a page directory. A free list tracks available pages or positions to support insertion.</p><p>For fixed-length records, a packed page places records next to one another, while an unpacked page may use a bitmap to mark occupied and empty positions. For variable-length records, a slotted page uses slots that record each record’s location and length. A record identifier (RID) in this slotted-page model is a pair: (page ID, slot ID).</p><p>Within a record, fixed-length fields can be located using calculated offsets. Variable-length fields need delimiters or offset/length metadata, together with a representation for NULL. This information locates fields inside a record. It serves a different purpose from the slot directory, which locates records inside a page.</p>"},{"id":"l6-2","title":"An original exercise: calculating page capacity","html":"<p>A page contains 1024 bytes, its header occupies 24 bytes, and each record is 50 bytes long. First subtract the header: 1024−24=1000 bytes remain. Dividing 1000 by 50 gives 20 records. In compact notation, floor((1024−24)/50)=20. The floor operation rounds down so that we count only records that fit completely inside the page. Assume there is no other metadata or padding.</p><p>The seventh record follows the header and the first six records. Numbering record positions from 1, its offset is 24+(7−1)×50=324 bytes from the start of the page.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Record capacity and record position require different calculations.</b> Capacity uses the bytes left after the header. The offset of the seventh record must skip six preceding records, rather than seven.</p></aside><p>Now add a slot directory with 4 bytes per slot. Each record and its slot require 50+4=54 bytes in total. Capacity falls to floor(1000/54)=18 records. These consume 18×54=972 bytes, leaving 28 bytes. The slot directory adds metadata, but it allows a record’s identifier to remain unchanged when its byte position moves.</p><details><summary>What if each record is 60 bytes long?</summary><p>With a 4-byte slot, floor(1000/64)=15 records fit. They occupy 960 bytes and leave 40 bytes unused. Rounding up would place part of the last record beyond the page boundary.</p></details>"},{"id":"l6-3","title":"Moving bytes without changing the RID","html":"<p><strong>An original page state.</strong> Page 9 contains slot 0→offset 900,length 40; slot 1→offset 840,length 60; and slot 2→offset 790,length 50. An index points to RID (9,2). A lookup first reaches page 9, then reads slot 2 to discover that the record is currently at offset 790.</p><p>Deleting the record in slot 1 leaves bytes 840–899 free. Compaction moves records together to consolidate free space. Move the record in slot 2 from offset 790 to offset 850, then update slot 2→850. Its length is 50 bytes, so it occupies [850,900), meaning bytes 850 through 899. It now sits immediately before the record in slot 0, which starts at 900. Choosing 850=900−50 leaves no gap between those two records.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Moving a record does not necessarily change its RID.</b> The index still stores (9,2). Its next lookup reads slot 2 and obtains the new offset, 850. If we instead changed the record’s slot ID from 2 to 1, the old index entry would no longer identify it correctly.</p></aside><p>A deletion may mark a slot as empty. Rules for safely reusing slot identifiers over time are an implementation issue outside this exercise.</p>"},{"id":"l6-4","title":"Designing a record layout and representing NULL","html":"<p>Use this simple record model for practice: a 4-byte id, a 2-byte name offset, a 2-byte name length, a 1-byte NULL bitmap, then the name’s UTF-8 bytes. For the ASCII name “Linh”, the fixed part occupies 4+2+2+1=9 bytes, so the name offset is 9 bytes from the start of the record. The name occupies 4 bytes: length=4, and the full record occupies 9+4=13 bytes. Assume there is no padding.</p><p>In this model, one bit in the NULL bitmap describes the name: bit=0 means a value is present, and bit=1 means NULL. An empty string has length=0 and bit=0; a NULL name has bit=1. Therefore, length=0 alone cannot distinguish an empty string from NULL. For Vietnamese text, count UTF-8 bytes rather than assuming that each character occupies one byte.</p><p><strong>Independent extension exercise.</strong> The name grows from 4 to 20 bytes. The page lacks a sufficiently large contiguous gap, but its total free space is sufficient. Propose compacting the page and updating the slot offset. If even the total free space is insufficient, another policy is needed: a slot directory cannot create additional storage capacity.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"In the slotted-page example, must the index change the RID when the record in slot 2 moves?","options":["Yes, always","No, if the slot ID remains unchanged","The RID is a byte offset, so the record cannot move"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slot directory updates the byte offset. The index still finds the record through (page 9, slot 2)."},"source":{"summary":"Files/records API; heap page lists and directories; fixed packed/unpacked layouts; slotted pages; fixed/variable fields and NULL metadata.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=4","label":"PDF pp. 4–9 / slides 4–9 · files and heaps"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=11","label":"PDF pp. 11–22 / slides 11–22 · page layout"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=24","label":"PDF pp. 24–29 / slides 24–29 · record bytes"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–29","pdfPageCount":29,"topics":["Logical file/API","Heap linked list","Page directory","Packed records","Bitmap/free list","Slotted page","RID","Fixed fields","Variable fields","NULL bitmap"],"limitations":[],"week":2,"officialTitle":"Files, Pages & Records","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-2","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"O VERVIEW : R EPRESENTATIONS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"F ILES OF PAGES OF R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"F ILES OF PAGES OF R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"DB F ILE O RGANISATION"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"H EAP F ILE"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : L INKED L IST"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : PAGE D IRECTORY"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"PAGE L AYOUT"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , U NPACKED (B ITMAP )"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , U NPACKED (F REE L IST )"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , U NPACKED (F REE L IST )"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"S LOTTED PAGES"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"S LOTTED PAGES"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"R ECORD L AYOUT"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"R ECORD L AYOUT : F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"storage"},{"id":"lecture-07","lecture":7,"title":"Storage models and compression","short":"Row, column, PAX & encodings","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"A useful physical layout depends on which parts of the data the workload actually reads.","goals":["Compare row, column, and PAX layouts by the bytes read","Encode and decode RLE, delta, and dictionary representations","Distinguish a teaching model from the actual Parquet format"],"sections":[{"id":"l7-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>Updating one order is an example of an OLTP workload, or online transaction processing. A report that totals revenue across millions of orders is an OLAP workload, or online analytical processing. HTAP combines transactional and analytical requirements. The storage layout should suit the parts of the data that each workload usually reads.</p><p>A row layout places the fields of one record next to each other. A column layout places many values from the same column together. PAX groups values by column within each page. Parquet organises data into row groups, then column chunks within each group, with pages and metadata inside that structure.</p><p>Lossless compression allows the original data to be recovered exactly. Compression can reduce I/O, but compression and decompression require CPU work. Techniques include general-purpose compression, run-length encoding (RLE), delta encoding, bit packing, and dictionary encoding. The examples below work from an original sequence to its encoded values, including subtracting the minimum delta and choosing a bit width for the residuals.</p>"},{"id":"l7-2","title":"An original exercise: which bytes enter RAM?","html":"<p>There are 1,000,000 records of 100 bytes each. A report uses only two fields whose combined size is 12 bytes. Ignore headers, compression, and alignment padding. During a full scan, a row-oriented layout reads approximately 1,000,000×100=100,000,000 bytes, or 100 MB. A column-oriented layout can read just the two required columns: 1,000,000×12=12,000,000 bytes, or 12 MB. Here, 1 MB=1,000,000 bytes.</p><p>The reduction is 100−12=88 MB, or 88% of the original 100 MB. The ratio of bytes read is 100/12≈8.33.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A byte-count ratio of 8.33 does not guarantee an 8.33-fold speedup.</b> The byte-count ratio in this example is approximately 8.33, but execution also includes reconstructing rows from separate columns, decompression, filtering, and other CPU work. The ratio describes the bytes read under the stated assumptions.</p></aside><p>If a query retrieves one complete record by id, gathering its fields from separate columns may take more work than reading one contiguous record. For practice, arrange six rows into two row groups of three rows each. Within each group, write column A’s values together, followed by column B’s and then column C’s. Your drawing illustrates a hybrid layout: within each row group, values from the same column sit together.</p>"},{"id":"l7-3","title":"Original encodings you can decode by hand","html":"<p><strong>Run-length encoding (RLE).</strong> Start with [7,7,7,2,2,9]. Group each consecutive run of equal values and store (value,count): [(7,3),(2,2),(9,1)]. Compute the sum directly from the runs: 7×3+2×2+9=34. The alternating sequence [1,2,1,2] has no long repeated runs, so metadata may make its encoded representation larger than the original.</p><p><strong>Delta encoding.</strong> The original sequence is [50,53,54,58,59]. Instead of storing every number in full, retain the first value and the difference between each adjacent pair:</p><ol><li>Keep first=50. Calculate 53−50=3, 54−53=1, 58−54=4, and 59−58=1, giving deltas=[3,1,4,1].</li><li>The smallest delta is minDelta=1. Subtract 1 from each delta to obtain residual=[2,0,3,0]. A residual is what remains after subtracting the minimum delta.</li><li>The largest residual is 3. Two bits represent the unsigned values 0–3, so each residual fits in 2 bits. Decoding still requires first, minDelta, and any necessary metadata.</li><li>Add minDelta back to each residual: [2,0,3,0]+1 → [3,1,4,1]. Start at 50 and cumulatively add 3,1,4,1: 50→53→54→58→59.</li></ol><p><strong>Dictionary encoding.</strong> The sequence [tea,cake,tea,juice] contains three distinct values. Store the dictionary tea→0,cake→1,juice→2. Replacing each value with its code gives [0,1,0,2]. Decode by looking up each code in that same dictionary.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Dictionary codes do not automatically preserve the original values’ order.</b> Here tea has code 0 and cake has code 1, but cake comes before tea alphabetically. Comparing codes as ordered values is valid only when the code assignment preserves the required order.</p></aside>"},{"id":"l7-4","title":"Capacity practice and the limits of the model","html":"<p>Suppose 1,000 values are all in the range 0–7. The bit-packed payload requires 1000×3=3000 bits, or 375 bytes, compared with 4000 bytes if every number occupies 32 bits. This calculation excludes headers and padding.</p><details><summary>Check yourself: what changes if the value 8 appears?</summary><p>The range 0–8 requires 4 bits per value if the entire block uses one fixed width. The payload becomes 500 bytes. A single exceptional value can change which encoding is best.</p></details><p>These calculations are independently designed exercises, not a standards-compliant Parquet encoder. PDF pages 19–20 introduce the format’s structure; implementing a real parser requires its current specification. Do not assume that every columnar API uses the same file format.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"What is the minimum number of bits per unsigned value needed to encode residual=[2,0,3,0]?","options":["1","2","3"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The largest value is 3, represented as binary 11, which fits in 2 bits."},"source":{"summary":"OLTP/OLAP/HTAP; row/column/PAX; Parquet organisation and metadata; lossless compression trade-offs; RLE, delta, bit packing, dictionary.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–16 / slides 2–17 · workloads and storage"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=17","label":"PDF pp. 17–20 / slides 18–21 · PAX/Parquet"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pp. 21–28 / slides 22–29 · compression"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–28","pdfPageCount":28,"topics":["OLTP/OLAP/HTAP","Row store","Column store","PAX","Parquet hierarchy","Metadata pruning","Compression tradeoff","Generic codecs","RLE","Delta/min-delta","Bit packing","Dictionary"],"limitations":["Parquet exercise is conceptual, not a specification-conformant implementation.","Printed slide 16 is absent; PDF page 16 is slide 17."],"week":3,"officialTitle":"Storage Models & Compression","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-3","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"DATABASE W ORKLOADS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"OLTP: ON-LINE TRANSACTIONAL PROCESSING"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"OLAP: O N -L INE A NALYTICAL P ROCESSING"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"O BSERVATION"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"S TORAGE M ODELS"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"H YBRID S TORAGE M ODEL (PAX)"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"H YBRID S TORAGE M ODEL                                              Col A      Col B      Col C"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"PARQUET F ILE F ORMAT"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"PARQUET F ILE F ORMAT"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"COMPRESSION IN DBMS"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"N AÏVE COMPRESSION"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"COLUMNAR COMPRESSION"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"D ELTA E NCODING IN PARQUET"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"D ELTA E NCODING IN PARQUET"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"D ELTA E NCODING IN PARQUET"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"D ICTIONARY E NCODING"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"CONCLUSION"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":7},"lab":"storage"},{"id":"lecture-08","lecture":8,"title":"File organisations and index types","short":"Heap, sorted, clustered & unclustered","group":"03 · Indexing","time":25,"lead":"Before choosing a storage structure, define the workload, the queries and updates it must support, and the cost model.","goals":["Compare heap and sorted-file costs under the same assumptions","Distinguish a search key, a primary key, and an index entry","Calculate the data-fetch costs of clustered and unclustered indexes"],"sections":[{"id":"l8-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>To find a record, we can scan a file, search a sorted file, or use an index to identify where to read. A heap file does not require records to follow search-key order. A sorted file keeps the data ordered by its sorting column. This lesson compares scans, equality searches for one value, range searches, insertions, and deletions using a simple page-I/O model.</p><p>A search key is the column or group of columns an index uses for lookup. Unlike a primary key, it need not be unique. An index entry can contain a record, a record identifier (RID), or a list of RIDs. Tree versus hash, clustered versus unclustered, and primary versus secondary describe different aspects of an index.</p>"},{"id":"l8-2","title":"An original cost comparison","html":"<p>Consider a file with 1024 pages. In this exercise’s model, reading or writing one page takes 2 ms. Let P=1024 be the number of pages and D=2 ms be the time per page I/O. R denotes the number of records per page if we need to convert between record counts and page counts. The calculations below use P and D.</p><p>Assume a cold cache: none of the required data is already in RAM. The search key occurs exactly once and is equally likely to be on any page. For heap insertion, the location of the final page is known, and that page has free space. The sorted file is stored contiguously without reserved gaps, and the search uses the column on which the file is sorted. Ignore CPU costs and differences between random and sequential I/O.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Binary search requires the file to follow the search order.</b> A file sorted by product code does not support binary search by price. The logarithmic cost below applies only when the search condition matches the sorting column.</p></aside><ul><li><strong>Full scan:</strong> read all 1024 pages. Both organisations cost P×D=1024×2=2048 ms.</li><li><strong>Heap equality search:</strong> read approximately half the file, giving P/2×D=512×2=1024 ms. The exact expected number of pages under these assumptions is (P+1)/2=512.5, corresponding to 1025 ms.</li><li><strong>Sorted equality search:</strong> binary search repeatedly halves the search region. Since log₂1024=10, the approximate model cost is log₂1024×2=20 ms.</li><li><strong>Heap insertion:</strong> read the final page, modify it in RAM, and write it back. Two I/Os cost approximately 2×2=4 ms.</li><li><strong>Sorted insertion:</strong> locating the insertion position costs approximately 10 I/Os. Assume that, on average, half the file must shift: 512 pages, each read and written once, require 512×2=1024 I/Os. The total is (10+1024)×2=2068 ms.</li></ul><p>These are model-based approximations using a common set of assumptions to compare file organisations. The figure of 20 ms is not a prediction of elapsed time for a real database.</p>"},{"id":"l8-3","title":"Original index entries and data locality","html":"<p>Product(pid,category,price) contains three records: (1,tea,20),(2,tea,25),(3,cake,30). The search key category is not unique even though pid is unique. Looking up tea must lead to both products in that category.</p><ul><li>A record-containing entry has the form (tea, complete record).</li><li>RID entries have the form (tea,rid1),(tea,rid2), with one entry pointing to each record.</li><li>A list entry has the form (tea,[rid1,rid2]), grouping the RIDs that share one search-key value.</li></ul><p>With a clustered index, the data order follows the search-key order, so records close in key order tend to occupy fewer pages. An unclustered index may point to records scattered across many pages. Suppose a query matches 80 records and each data page holds 20 records. If the matches fit exactly on four pages, 80/20=4 page reads are sufficient for the data-fetch portion of the query. If each matching record is on a different page and the cache is cold, that portion may require 80 reads.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Add the cost of locating index entries to the cost of fetching records.</b> The four or 80 reads above count only data pages. Also count the path through the index and the leaf pages containing its entries when those pages are not already cached.</p></aside>"},{"id":"l8-4","title":"Practise choosing a design","html":"<p>Consider three new workloads. A always outputs the entire table. B looks up a code and updates one record. C retrieves every transaction within a date range. Suggest one design and one disadvantage for each workload.</p><details><summary>Suggestions, rather than unique answers</summary><p>For A, a heap file is simple, but it does not guarantee an output order. For B, an index that supports equality lookup narrows the search, but it must be maintained when the indexed data changes. For C, a tree index on the date supports range queries. Clustering can reduce the number of data pages fetched, but maintaining that layout can make updates more expensive.</p></details><p>Do not choose a design based only on the word “index”. Two indexes with the same tree structure can have very different data-fetch costs if one is clustered and the other is unclustered. Base your conclusion on selectivity, the fraction of records that match the condition, as well as the cache and record layout.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"The 80 matching records occupy four data pages. How many page reads fetch those records if none of the pages is cached?","options":["4, excluding index traversal","Always 80","0, because an index exists"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Count the pages that actually need to be read. Several records on the same page do not require a separate page read for each record."},"source":{"summary":"I/O-only file cost model; heap/sorted operations; index entry variants; search keys; tree/hash; clustering, primary/secondary and uniqueness.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=5","label":"PDF pp. 5–19 / slides 5–21 · cost model"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pp. 20–29 / slides 22–33 · index concepts"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=30","label":"PDF pp. 30–34 / slides 36–49 · clustering"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–34","pdfPageCount":34,"topics":["P/R/D assumptions","Heap costs","Sorted costs","Range search","Insert/delete","Search key","Entry variants A/B/C","Tree/hash","Primary/secondary","Unique","Clustered/unclustered"],"limitations":["Printed slide numbers have gaps; final PDF page 34 is printed slide 49.","Costs are simplified approximations under stated assumptions."],"week":3,"officialTitle":"File Organisations","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-3","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"R ECAP : F ILE O RGANISATIONS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"C OMPARING F ILE O RGANISATIONS"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"G OALS"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"C OST M ODEL FOR A NALYSIS"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"R ECORD O PERATIONS"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"H EAP F ILES"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : S CAN A LL R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : S EARCH ON K EY"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : S EARCH ON N ON -K EY"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : R ANGE S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : I NSERT & D ELETE"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"H EAP F ILE A NALYSIS"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"S ORTED F ILES"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE : S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE : S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE : I NSERT & D ELETE"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE A NALYSIS"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"H EAP F ILE VS . S ORTED F ILE"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"I NTRODUCTION TO I NDEXES"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"I NDEX U SAGE"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"I NDEX E NTRIES"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"I NDEX E NTRIES"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"I NDEXING BY R EFERENCE"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"I NDEX C LASSIFICATION"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":31,"heading":"B+ T REE I NDEXES"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":32,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":33,"heading":"H ASH -B ASED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":36,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":31,"printedSlide":37,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":32,"printedSlide":39,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":33,"printedSlide":40,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":34,"printedSlide":49,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":9},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-09","lecture":9,"title":"Tree indexing: ISAM and B+ trees","short":"Search, split, merge & invariants","group":"03 · Indexing","time":25,"lead":"Leaves store data entries. Internal nodes use separator keys to choose a search branch. The same value can appear in both places with different roles.","goals":["Use separators to find a leaf and scan a range","Simulate insertion and deletion using the order-d convention","Explain copy-up, push-up, and byte-based occupancy"],"sections":[{"id":"l9-1","title":"The conceptual framework","html":"<p>ISAM keeps its internal-node structure fixed. When a leaf runs out of space, it adds an overflow page to hold extra entries. A B+ tree balances dynamically: all leaves are at the same depth, links connect leaves for ordered scans, and updates may split nodes, redistribute entries, or merge nodes.</p><p>Fanout is the number of children of a node. Under this lesson’s order-d convention, a non-root leaf contains d to 2d data entries. A non-root internal node contains d to 2d separator keys. It has one more child pointer than separator keys, giving d+1 to 2d+1 children. A separator is a boundary value used to choose a search branch. The root has exceptions to the minimum occupancy rule. The lesson also considers fanout, caching upper levels, practical deletion policies, occupancy with variable-length entries, prefix compression, and suffix truncation.</p>"},{"id":"l9-2","title":"An original insertion: copying a leaf key upwards","html":"<p>Choose d=2, so each leaf holds at most 2d=4 entries. The root is currently the leaf [4,9,12,18]. Inserting 15 in order produces [4,9,12,15,18]. Its five entries exceed the leaf’s capacity.</p><ol><li>Split it into a left leaf [4,9] and a right leaf [12,15,18]. They contain two and three entries respectively, both within the permitted range from d=2 to 2d=4.</li><li>Create a new root with separator [12] and pointers to the two leaves. Keys &lt;12 go left; keys ≥12 go right.</li><li>Keep 12 in the right leaf. The copy of 12 in the root only selects the search branch. This operation is called copy-up.</li><li>Connect the leaves with next/prev links so an ordered scan can move between them.</li></ol><p>Searching for 15 follows the ≥12 branch and finds 15 in the right leaf. Searching for 10 follows the &lt;12 branch and finds no matching entry. To scan the inclusive range 8..16, start in the left leaf, retain 9, then follow its link to collect 12 and 15 from the right leaf. The result is [9,12,15].</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Copy-up retains the data key in the leaf.</b> The root’s copy of 12 is used for routing. The leaf must retain 12 so that a scan through the leaves reads all the data entries.</p></aside><p><strong>A separate internal-node split with new data.</strong> An internal node temporarily contains keys [10,20,30,40,50] and six children. Push the middle separator, 30, up into the parent. The resulting internal nodes contain [10,20] and [40,50], with three children each. Unlike a leaf split, 30 no longer appears as a separator in either of those two nodes. Push-up moves a separator; it does not delete a data entry from a leaf.</p>"},{"id":"l9-3","title":"An original deletion: redistribute, then merge","html":"<p>Start with the tree produced by the leaf split. Delete 4: the left leaf becomes [9], with only one entry, below the minimum d=2. The right leaf [12,15,18] has three entries, so it can lend one while retaining the required two. Move 12 to the left, producing [9,12] and [15,18]. Change the root separator from 12 to 15, the smallest key in the right leaf under this example’s convention.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Redistributing a key also requires updating the parent’s separator.</b> If the root still stores 12, a search for 12 goes right even though the record has moved left. Valid leaf occupancy alone does not guarantee correct lookup.</p></aside><p>Now delete 9. The left leaf contains [12], while the right leaf has exactly two entries and cannot lend one without falling below its minimum. Merge the leaves into [12,15,18], which fits within the four-entry capacity. The root now has no separator and only one child, so replace the root with that leaf. The tree becomes one level shorter.</p><p>After each step, check the invariants: in this unique-key example, every data entry occurs exactly once in the leaves; all leaves have the same depth; separators divide key ranges correctly; and occupancy is valid apart from the root’s permitted exception. A tree that merely looks balanced may still route a lookup incorrectly.</p>"},{"id":"l9-4","title":"ISAM, byte capacity, and an open exercise","html":"<p><strong>An original example.</strong> An ISAM leaf for the range [20,40) is full. Inserting 27,28,29 may create an overflow chain while leaving the root unchanged. Under the same workload, a B+ tree may change its structure through splitting. Its maintenance costs differ, but adding data does not necessarily increase its height.</p><p>With variable-length string keys, three long entries may fill a page that could hold ten short entries. In a byte-based simulation, set capacity=256 bytes and include key bytes, pointers, and the header. Do not automatically equate half of the entry count with half of the byte capacity. This is a modelling exercise, not a policy shared by every database engine.</p><details><summary>With fanout=100 and 1,000,000 leaf pages, how many edges connect the root to a leaf in an ideal full tree?</summary><p>log₁₀₀(1,000,000)=3 edges, giving 4 levels when both the root and the leaves are counted. Actual page I/O also depends on which levels are cached and what the leaf entries contain.</p></details>"}],"quiz":{"question":"When the leaf [4,9,12,15,18] splits into [4,9] and [12,15,18], what happens to separator 12?","options":["It is removed from every leaf","It is copied into the parent and retained in the right leaf","It is retained only in the left leaf"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leaves store data entries. The parent’s copy is used for routing, so copy-up retains the original entry in the leaf."},"source":{"summary":"ISAM and overflow; fanout; B+ tree search and invariants; leaf/inner splits; redistribution/merge; deletion practice; variable-length occupancy and key compression.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–12 / slides 2–12 · ISAM/fanout"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pp. 13–20 / slides 13–20 · B+ tree search and insertion"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pp. 21–29 / slides 21–29 · deletion and optimisations"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–30","pdfPageCount":30,"topics":["Sorted/binary search","Multilevel index","ISAM overflow","Fanout","B+ invariants","Range leaf links","Leaf copy-up","Inner push-up","Redistribution","Merge/root collapse","Deletion in practice","Byte occupancy","Prefix/suffix compression"],"limitations":["Order d is the lecture convention; other materials define order differently.","Tree simulator examples enforce textbook invariants, unlike some practical lazy-deletion policies."],"week":3,"officialTitle":"Tree-Structured Indexing","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-3","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"S ORTED F ILES AND B INARY S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"S ORTED F ILES AND B INARY S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"T REE -S TRUCTURED I NDEXING"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"T REE -S TRUCTURED I NDEXING"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"ISAM: I NDEXED S EQUENTIAL A CCESS M ETHOD"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"ISAM: I NDEXED S EQUENTIAL A CCESS M ETHOD"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"E XAMPLE ISAM T REE"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"A FTER I NSERTING 23*, 48*, 41*, 42*…"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"… T HEN D ELETING 42*, 97*, 51*"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"C OMMENTS ON ISAM"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"ISAM VS . B INARY S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"B+ T REE : M OST W IDELY U SED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"B+ T REES IN P RACTICE"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"I NSERTING A D ATA E NTRY"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"I NSERTING 8* INTO E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE A FTER I NSERTING 8*"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"R EDISTRIBUTION : I NSERTING 6*"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"D ELETING A D ATA E NTRY FROM A B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"D ELETING 19* AND 20*…"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"… A ND T HEN D ELETING 24*"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"… S TILL D ELETING 24* (M ERGE )"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"… S TILL D ELETING 24* (R EDISTRIBUTION )"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"B+ T REE : D ELETIONS"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"VARIABLE L ENGTH K EYS & R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"R EDEFINE O CCUPANCY I NVARIANT"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"O PTIMIZATIONS"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-10","lecture":10,"title":"Hash-Based Indexing","short":"Hash indexes: buckets and directories","group":"03 · Indexing","time":25,"lead":"Follow how a hash index assigns keys to buckets, then see how the buckets and directory change as data is added.","goals":["Explain in-memory hashing and collision resolution","Trace searches, insertions, deletions, and overflow in static chained hashing","Recognise skew and identify when rehashing is needed","Simulate global and local depth, bucket splits, and directory changes in extendible hashing"],"sections":[{"id":"l10-s1","title":"Ask the right questions of a hash index","html":"<p>A hash index applies a hash function to assign each key to a bucket, a storage area for entries with the same hash address. Two keys in the same bucket must still be compared directly. This approach suits equality lookups, which search for a key with a particular value. For a range query, keys that are close in value need not be close together after hashing.</p><p><strong>Original worked example:</strong> divide each ticket code by 4 and use the remainder as its bucket number. In compact notation, h(k)=k mod 4, where k is the ticket code and h(k) is a bucket number from 0 to 3. Codes 6, 10, and 14 all enter bucket 2. Finding code 10 requires checking the entries in that bucket; finding every code from 6 to 14 cannot be done by reading bucket 2 alone. Chaining links entries into a list; open addressing searches for another slot in an in-memory table. In a database, a bucket commonly occupies one or more pages.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>The same hash does not mean the same key.</b> Hashing narrows down where to look; key comparisons are still needed to confirm a match.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–6</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l10-s2","title":"Static hashing: overflow determines the cost","html":"<p>Keep a fixed set of 4 primary bucket pages, each holding 2 entries. Inserting 6 and 10 fills bucket 2; inserting 14 requires an overflow page, an additional page linked to a full bucket. Looking up 14 may read 2 pages even though the hash function runs only once. Insertions and deletions must also write any modified pages. As the data grows, overflow chains lengthen; as it shrinks, some buckets may be left empty.</p><p>Rehashing recalculates the location of every entry using a new bucket mapping. Increasing the number of buckets redistributes the data but requires many page reads and writes. A fast hash function is not enough: a skewed key distribution or many duplicate keys can still create hot spots. The modulo function above is an illustration, not the best choice for every dataset.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 7–12</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l10-s3","title":"Extendible hashing: grow where space is needed","html":"<p>Extendible hashing uses a directory, a table of pointers to buckets. The global depth g is the number of hash bits used to look up a directory entry; the local depth d is the number of bits that identify a particular bucket. The example below uses the trailing bits, or suffix.</p><p><strong>Original worked example:</strong> g=2 gives 4 directory pointers labelled 00, 01, 10, and 11. Bucket 01 has d=2, holds at most 2 entries, and currently contains hash values 1 (001) and 5 (101). Inserting 9 (1001) exceeds its capacity, so split it using the last 3 bits. Bucket 001 receives 1 and 9; bucket 101 receives 5. Both new buckets have d=3, which exceeds the old g=2, so the directory grows from 4 to 8 pointers.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Doubling the directory enlarges only the pointer table.</b> This step splits the full bucket; it does not copy every data bucket.</p></aside><p>If the bucket has d&lt;g before splitting, the directory already has enough bits to distinguish the two new buckets, so only the relevant pointers change. For example, with g=3 and d=1, the remaining 2 bits have 4 combinations, so 4 pointers refer to that bucket. In general, the number of pointers is 2^(g−d). Deletion may allow buckets to merge and the directory to shrink when the structural conditions permit it.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 13–25</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l10-s4","title":"Check your understanding with a trace","html":"<p>With global depth g=3 and a bucket whose local depth is d=1, how many pointers refer to that bucket? Does splitting it once require doubling the directory?</p><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><p>There are 2^(3−1)=4 pointers. After the split, each new bucket has d=2 and receives 2 pointers. No doubling is needed because 2≤3. If several keys have exactly the same complete hash value, adding bits cannot separate them; a practical implementation needs a separate way to handle this case.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 15–25</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"A bucket with local depth 2 and global depth 4 is split into buckets with local depth 3. Which statement is correct?","options":["The directory does not need to double","The directory must double","Every bucket must be rehashed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The new local depth, 3, still does not exceed the global depth, 4."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage: In-memory hashing and collision resolution; Static chained hashing: search, insertion, deletion, and overflow; Hash functions, skew, and rehashing; Extendible hashing: global/local depth, splitting, doubling, and shrinking. The explanations and worked calculations above are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–12"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pages 13–25"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=26","label":"PDF pages 26–26"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–26","topics":["In-memory hashing and collision resolution","Static chained hashing: search, insertion, deletion, and overflow","Hash functions, skew, and rehashing","Extendible hashing: global/local depth, splitting, doubling, and shrinking"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-11","lecture":11,"title":"External Sorting & Aggregation","short":"Sorting when the data exceeds RAM","group":"04 · Query execution","time":25,"lead":"Count the reads and writes required when sorting or aggregation must process more data than fits in RAM.","goals":["Connect query execution to the implementation of individual operators","Calculate the number of passes in two-way and multiway external merge sort","Compare reading through clustered and unclustered B+ trees","Use sorting to implement DISTINCT"],"sections":[{"id":"l11-s1","title":"From ORDER BY to external merge sort","html":"<p>Sorting supports ORDER BY, DISTINCT, GROUP BY, merge joins, and bulk loading. With a multi-column key, lexicographic order compares the first column first and considers each later column only when the preceding columns are equal. When the data exceeds RAM, it must be divided into smaller pieces and read and written sequentially. Relying on virtual memory alone does not make an in-memory algorithm efficient.</p><p><strong>New worked example:</strong> sort 90 pages using 6 buffer frames allocated to the algorithm. Each frame holds one page.</p><ol><li>Pass 0: read 6 pages into RAM, sort them, and write a run, which is a sorted sequence. Repeat until all the data has been processed, producing 90/6=15 runs.</li><li>Reserve 5 frames for reading 5 runs and 1 frame for collecting output. Repeatedly take the smallest item among the 5 inputs, append it to the output, and advance that input. One merge pass combines the 15 runs into 3 runs.</li><li>The next merge pass combines the 3 runs into 1. The run counts are therefore 15→3→1, requiring 3 passes in total, including pass 0.</li></ol><p>Every pass reads 90 pages and writes 90 pages, giving 2×90×3=540 page I/Os. This count includes writing the final result to disk and excludes CPU cost.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–13</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l11-s2","title":"Count the passes and avoid unnecessary sorting","html":"<p>Let N be the number of pages to sort and B the number of buffer frames. Pass 0 processes up to B pages at a time, producing ceil(N/B) runs. Here ceil means rounding up, because a partial final batch still needs a run. Each merge pass combines at most B−1 runs because 1 frame must be reserved for output.</p><p>Repeat the merging until only 1 run remains. For N&gt;0 and B≥3, the total number of passes is 1+ceil(log_(B−1)(ceil(N/B))). The initial 1 counts pass 0; the logarithm counts the merges in groups of B−1, rounded up. If each pass reads and writes all the data, the cost is 2×N×the number of passes. For two-way merge sort that creates one-page runs and merges 2 runs at a time, the pass count is 1+ceil(log2 N).</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Count the runs and passes before substituting into a formula.</b> Pipelining the final output directly into the next operator may avoid the final write, but state that assumption before subtracting any I/O.</p></aside><p>A B+ tree provides key order. With a clustered index, records with nearby keys are also close together in the data file, making leaf-order access attractive. With an unclustered index, fetching complete records in index order may require one random fetch per tuple. Tuples and pages are different units.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 12–17</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l11-s3","title":"GROUP BY retains state, not necessarily every tuple","html":"<p><strong>Original worked example:</strong> sales(region,amount) contains (North,10), (South,8), and (North,14). To calculate AVG, retain a sum and row count for each group: North→(sum=24,count=2) and South→(8,1). Divide sum by count when producing the result, giving 12 and 8. MIN, MAX, SUM, and COUNT also have their own running state, so they do not require retaining every tuple.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>To combine averages, keep both the sum and the count.</b> When two groups have different row counts, averaging their two averages does not give the combined average.</p></aside><p>If the hash table of groups does not fit in RAM, read the data and partition it onto disk by group key. Then process each partition using a different hash function that is independent of the partitioning function. With B buffer frames, the partitioning phase reserves 1 frame for input and B−1 frames for writing B−1 partitions.</p><p>In a simple capacity model, if each partition subsequently fits into about B pages of RAM, the two phases can process approximately B(B−1) pages. This is only an estimate: overhead and skew may reduce the practical capacity, while combining state early may reduce the data that must be retained. Repeated partitioning cannot separate tuples with identical group keys; the state for that group must be combined.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 19–32</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l11-s4","title":"Exercise: sorting or hashing?","html":"<p>Suppose a query needs DISTINCT customer_id without any ordering. The requirement then changes to DISTINCT customer_id ORDER BY customer_id. How does that affect your choice?</p><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><p>Hashing is a candidate for the first query because it only needs to eliminate duplicate keys. The second query requires order, so sorting can both bring duplicate keys together for elimination and produce the required output order. Costs still need to be compared; no choice is best for every dataset.</p><p>Repeat the calculation with 90 pages and 10 buffer frames. Pass 0 produces 90/10=9 runs. Reserving 1 output frame leaves 9 input frames, so all 9 runs can be merged in one merge pass. There are 2 passes in total, costing 2×90×2=360 I/Os if the final output is also written.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 18–32</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"For N=90 and B=6, how many I/Os does multiway sorting require when the final output is written?","options":["360","540","900"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The run sequence 15→3→1 requires 3 passes, each costing 180 I/Os."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage: Query execution and operator implementations; Lexicographic sorting, two-way and multiway external merge sort; B+ tree sorting: clustered/unclustered; Sort-based DISTINCT; External hashing, recursive partitioning, and skew; Hash aggregation, running state, and memory bounds. The explanations and worked calculations above are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–32"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=15","label":"PDF pages 15–20"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pages 21–32"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–32","topics":["Query execution and operator implementations","Lexicographic sorting, two-way and multiway external merge sort","B+ tree sorting: clustered/unclustered","Sort-based DISTINCT","External hashing, recursive partitioning, and skew","Hash aggregation, running state, and memory bounds"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"sorting"},{"id":"lecture-12","lecture":12,"title":"Joins","short":"Choose joins by their actual cost","group":"04 · Query execution","time":25,"lead":"Compare join algorithms using page I/O counts under the same data and buffer assumptions.","goals":["Identify join semantics and the assumptions behind I/O calculations","Calculate the costs of tuple, page, and block nested-loop joins","Calculate index nested-loop join costs with clustering","Handle duplicates and combine the final merge pass with a sort-merge join"],"sections":[{"id":"l12-s1","title":"Nested loops: change the unit of iteration","html":"<p>In a nested-loop join, the outer input drives the outer loop. The inner input is scanned again for each outer tuple, page, or block. Start with a block nested-loop join (BNLJ) example: R occupies 120 pages, S occupies 40 pages, and memory provides 12 buffer frames. Each frame holds one page.</p><ol><li>Choose R as the outer input and S as the inner input. Reserve 1 frame for reading S and 1 for collecting output, leaving 10 frames to hold a block of R.</li><li>Read the first 10 pages of R into RAM. Keep them there while scanning all 40 pages of S to find every pair that satisfies the join condition.</li><li>Replace them with the next 10 pages of R, then scan all of S again. R has 120/10=12 blocks, so repeat this process 12 times.</li></ol><p>Reading R costs 120 page reads in total, with each page read once. Reading S costs 12×40=480 page reads. The total is 120+480=600 page I/Os. This model assumes that the inner input is rescanned for every outer block and that all of S is not retained between scans. It excludes CPU cost and writes of the final output.</p><p>Now express the calculation as a formula: M+ceil(M/(B−2))×N. Here M is the number of outer pages, N is the number of inner pages, and B is the number of buffer frames. B−2 frames hold the outer block, and ceil(M/(B−2)) counts the outer blocks. Round up because even an incomplete final block still requires an inner scan. The formula uses B≥3, and the inputs in this example are nonempty.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Use the number of outer blocks to count inner scans.</b> With 12 frames, the outer block uses 10 frames. Each outer block triggers one complete read of the inner input.</p></aside><p>Reverse the roles: S as the outer input has 40/10=4 blocks, and each block scans all 120 pages of R. The cost is 40+ceil(40/10)×120=40+4×120=520 I/Os. If the outer input fits in B−2 frames, one block is enough and each input is read once. Calculate both outer choices because rounding can affect the result.</p><p>The two simpler variants use the same reasoning. Page nested loops holds one R page at a time and scans S 120 times: 120+120×40=4,920 I/Os, or M+MN in general. If R has 12,000 tuples, tuple nested loops scans S 12,000 times: 120+12,000×40=480,120 I/Os, or M+mN, where m is the number of outer tuples.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 4–11</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l12-s2","title":"Index nested loops: find every match","html":"<p>An index nested-loop join reads an outer tuple, extracts its join key, and uses an index on the inner input to find every matching tuple. The cost of a lookup includes both finding the index entries and reading those inner tuples. A clustered index may place many matches on a small number of pages; an unclustered index may require many random I/Os. The index must support the join condition being used.</p><p><strong>New worked example:</strong> after filtering, the outer input contains 25 tuples on 2 pages. Reading it costs 2 I/Os, and each unique-key lookup on the inner input costs 3 I/Os, including index access and the data fetch. There are 25 lookups, giving 2+25×3=77 I/Os. Counting 2+25 would require an additional assumption that reduces each lookup to 1 I/O.</p><p>In compact form, cost = M+m×C_lookup. M counts outer page reads, m counts outer tuples, and C_lookup is the I/O cost of finding all inner matches in one lookup. If costs vary by key, sum the individual lookups or use a suitable average. To isolate the effect of the probe count, hold the outer read cost at 2 I/Os and increase the number of probes to 2,500: the cost becomes 2+2,500×3=7,502 I/Os. This comparison deliberately holds the outer read cost fixed.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A lookup must account for every match.</b> An index does not automatically make a join cheap: cost also depends on the number of outer tuples, the number of matches, and the data pages that must be read.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 12–15</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l12-s3","title":"Sort-merge and hashing: two ways to bring matches together","html":"<p>A sort-merge join sorts both inputs by the join key, then compares their current keys. Advance the pointer on the side with the smaller key. When the keys are equal, produce every pair across the two equal-key groups: 3 copies in R and 4 in S produce 3×4=12 tuples.</p><p>If the sorted results are written to disk, add the cost of sorting each input and then rereading the M pages of R and N pages of S for the merge: sort(R)+sort(S)+M+N. This scan formula assumes duplicate groups can be handled without additional reads; very large groups may increase the cost. The final merge pass of sorting can be combined with the join if enough buffers are available to read the runs from both inputs.</p><p>An in-memory hash join keeps a hash table for the smaller input in RAM during its build phase, then probes it with tuples from the larger input. When RAM is insufficient, Grace hash join uses the same hash function to divide R and S into corresponding partition pairs. R_i only needs to join S_i, where i is the partition number.</p><p>Count the operations: reading both inputs costs M+N; writing the partitions costs M+N; and rereading them for build and probe costs M+N. The total is 3(M+N), or 3×(120+40)=480 I/Os in this example. The formula excludes output writes and applies only when each build partition, including hash-table overhead, fits in RAM without further partitioning. Hash collisions still require checking key equality.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 16–41</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l12-s4","title":"Exercise: choose under explicit assumptions","html":"<p>For R=120 pages, S=40 pages, and the 12 buffer frames above, compare the best BNLJ choice with Grace hash join. What changes if both inputs are already sorted and the output must follow the join-key order?</p><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><p>The lower BNLJ cost across the two orientations is 520 I/Os. Grace hash join costs 480 I/Os if each build partition fits in RAM and the distribution is suitable. With already-sorted inputs, a merge scan needs only 120+40=160 page reads and may preserve the required order, assuming duplicate groups cause no rereading. For a general inequality condition, the equijoin hash formula does not apply; nested loops remains applicable.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 31–43</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Two groups with the same key contain 3 and 4 tuples. How many joined tuples do they produce?","options":["4","7","12"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A join retains every matching pair: 3×4=12. Each tuple in the first group matches every tuple in the second; the join does not automatically eliminate duplicate occurrences."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage: Join semantics and I/O assumptions; Tuple/page/block nested loops; Index nested loops and clustering; Sort-merge, duplicates, and final-pass fusion; In-memory hash join and Grace hash join; Recursive partitioning and algorithm trade-offs. The explanations and worked calculations above are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–43"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–15"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=16","label":"PDF pages 16–33"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=34","label":"PDF pages 34–43"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–43","topics":["Join semantics and I/O assumptions","Tuple/page/block nested loops","Index nested loops and clustering","Sort-merge, duplicates, and final-pass fusion","In-memory hash join and Grace hash join","Recursive partitioning and algorithm trade-offs"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-13","lecture":13,"title":"Query Evaluation: Processing Models","short":"One tuple, one batch, or the whole result?","group":"04 · Query execution","time":25,"lead":"Compare how operators pass individual tuples, batches, or complete intermediate results to one another.","goals":["Trace open, next, and close calls in the iterator model","Explain the state maintained by a selection, heap scan, and nested-loop join","Identify blocking operations such as sorting and hash-table construction","Explain pipelining, LIMIT, and iterator overhead"],"sections":[{"id":"l13-s1","title":"Iterators give operators a shared interface","html":"<p>In the iterator model, operators share three operations: open() initializes their processing state, next() returns one tuple or EOF to signal the end of the input, and close() releases their resources. This common interface lets a parent operator request tuples from a child without needing to know whether that child is a heap scan or a complex join. Calls travel down from the root, and tuples are returned upward.</p><p><strong>Original worked trace:</strong> Scan contains [2,7,4,9], Filter keeps x&gt;5, and Project calculates x×10. On the first call to root.next(), Filter requests 2 and discards it, then requests 7 and returns it. Project returns 70. On the second call, Filter discards 4 and keeps 9, so Project returns 90. The third call returns EOF.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>One parent next() call may invoke the child’s next() method several times.</b> A filter must keep requesting input until it finds a qualifying tuple or reaches EOF.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–6</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l13-s2","title":"State and blocking boundaries","html":"<p>A heap scan remembers its current page and slot. A nested-loop join remembers the current outer tuple and its position in the inner input. When the inner input is exhausted, the join restarts it and advances to the next outer tuple. A sort must inspect all of an unordered input before it knows the smallest item. An external sort may create runs during open() and merge them during next(). A hash join must finish building its hash table before it probes that table.</p><p>Pipelining passes results to the next operator as they become available, rather than writing and then rereading the whole intermediate result. It often reduces intermediate writes and the delay before the first output tuple. A blocking operator, such as a sort, still has to complete a preparatory phase before returning results.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>LIMIT 1 above a sort may still require reading the entire input.</b> To determine the first row in global order, the sort must consider rows that have not yet been read. A left-deep plan shape does not remove this blocking requirement.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 7–13</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l13-s3","title":"Vectorization and materialization","html":"<p><strong>Original worked example:</strong> pass 100,000 tuples through 3 operators, with no operator reducing the tuple count. Tuple-at-a-time processing needs approximately 100,000×3=300,000 calls to pass tuples. With 1,000 tuples per batch, each operator processes only 100 batches, giving approximately 100×3=300 batch-passing calls. This count excludes EOF calls. If a filter is present, the number of later batches also depends on its output.</p><p>Batch processing spreads the cost of a call over many tuples, supports tight processing loops, and may use SIMD to process several values at once. Materialization instead retains an operator’s entire output before moving to the next step. This can improve locality by accessing nearby data in memory, but a large intermediate result puts pressure on RAM and I/O. Iterators, batches, and materialization are execution choices; each must preserve the result required by the query.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 14–18</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l13-s4","title":"Exercise: identify the pipeline segments","html":"<p>Consider Scan → Filter → Sort → Project → LIMIT 2. Which segments can stream their tuples to the next operator? Where can processing stop early and release resources?</p><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><p>Scan→Filter can stream. Sort blocks the first result until it has prepared enough data. After that, Sort→Project→LIMIT can stream. Once it has received 2 rows, LIMIT can stop requesting further tuples and call close() down the tree. Any temporary runs still need to be cleaned up. A top-k algorithm is an additional optimization outside this example; do not silently assume it is being used.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 9–13</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Can one Filter.next() call invoke child.next() more than once?","options":["Yes, to discard tuples that do not satisfy the predicate","No, it always calls the child exactly once","Only when the child is an index"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The filter continues reading input until it finds a qualifying tuple or reaches EOF."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage includes the open/next/close iterator interface and composability; selection, heap-scan, and nested-loop state; blocking sorts and hash builds; pipelining, LIMIT, and iterator overhead; vectorized batch and SIMD processing; and materialization, execution direction, and workload trade-offs. The explanations and calculations are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–18"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–9"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pages 10–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=15","label":"PDF pages 15–18"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–18","topics":["Iterator open/next/close and composability","Selection, heap scan, nested-loop state","Blocking sort and hash build","Pipelining, LIMIT and iterator overhead","Vectorised batch/SIMD model","Materialisation, execution direction and workload trade-offs"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-14","lecture":14,"title":"Query Optimisation: Plan Space","short":"Which plans are valid candidates?","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Identify equivalent execution plans before comparing their estimated costs.","goals":["Distinguish the parser, rewriter, and cost-based optimizer","Describe query blocks and the System R approach","Apply logical equivalences and safe simplifications","Change join order while preserving predicates and handling cross products"],"sections":[{"id":"l14-s1","title":"Three different optimization decisions","html":"<p>Query optimization must answer three questions: which plans are equivalent (the plan space), how much each plan is expected to cost (cost estimation), and how to explore the candidates (search). Parsing and semantic analysis check the query and produce an internal representation. A rewriter transforms the query while preserving its result. The cost-based optimizer uses statistics to compare execution choices. Search time is limited, and costs are estimates.</p><p><strong>Original worked example:</strong> WHERE 2=3 is always false, so the system can produce an empty filtered input without scanning the data. Later operators must still be evaluated correctly: for example, COUNT(*) without GROUP BY returns one row containing 0 for that empty input. WHERE amount&gt;20 AND amount&gt;50 can be simplified to amount&gt;50. However, a join cannot be removed merely because the output uses no right-hand columns: multiple matches on the right can still increase the number of output rows.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A correct rewrite must preserve each row’s multiplicity.</b> Under SQL bag semantics, the same values with different numbers of copies constitute different results.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 1–3</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l14-s2","title":"Equivalence must preserve columns and predicates","html":"<p>For the deterministic relational predicates considered here, two filter conditions connected by AND can be split into two selections and reordered. Projection can be pushed down if it retains every column needed by later filters, joins, and output operations. Inner joins can be reordered, but each predicate must be checked where all the columns it uses are available. For example, a condition using a column from S cannot be evaluated in a subtree containing only R and T.</p><p><strong>Original worked example:</strong> consider Orders(customer_id,product_id), Customers(id,name,region), and Products(id,price). To find the names of customers who bought expensive products, Products can be filtered first. A projection on Orders must keep customer_id to join Customers and product_id to join Products. Forming Customers×Products first may still produce a correct final result if all predicates are subsequently applied, but it may generate a very large intermediate relation.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 4–4</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l14-s3","title":"Heuristics are useful, but do not guarantee the fastest plan","html":"<p>Pushing filters down often reduces cardinality, which is the number of rows. Pushing projections down reduces row width. Avoiding Cartesian products often reduces work. These are useful heuristics for choosing candidates, not guarantees of the fastest plan. An expensive CPU-bound filter, available indexes, and the cost of materializing temporary results can all change the comparison.</p><p>For equality predicates A=B and B=C, transitivity allows us to add A=C. This inferred predicate may enable additional join orders while preserving the result. At the physical level, an equijoin might use block nested loops, index nested loops, sort-merge, or hashing. Data may be accessed through a scan or an index. Before choosing, check whether the index has the right key, whether an input is already ordered, and whether the smaller input fits in RAM.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Inner-join reordering rules do not automatically apply to outer joins.</b> Outer joins also preserve unmatched rows. Moving a join or a filter can remove rows that should have been retained.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 4–7</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l14-s4","title":"Exercise: repair an invalid rewrite","html":"<p>Let R(a,x), S(a,b), and T(b,y) be joined using R.a=S.a and S.b=T.b. Someone rewrites (R join S) join T as R join (S join T), then removes R.a=S.a. Are the two plans still equivalent?</p><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><p>No. Changing the grouping of joins does not permit removing a join condition. The S join T subtree uses S.b=T.b; the top-level join must still check R.a=S.a. If S is projected early, both a and b must remain. List the columns available at each node before moving a predicate.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 4–6</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which columns must projection pushdown retain?","options":["Only the columns in the final SELECT list","Output columns and every column still needed by later operators","Only primary-key columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Join keys and filter columns remain necessary even when they are absent from the final SELECT list."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage includes the parser, rewriter, and cost-based optimizer; query blocks and the System R approach; logical equivalences and safe simplifications; join reordering, cross products, and transitive predicates; selection and projection pushdown; and physical implementation choices. The explanations and calculations are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–7 (2×2; slides 1–28, numbering contains gaps)"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–2"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–5"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=6","label":"PDF pages 6–7"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–7","topics":["Parser, rewriter, cost-based optimiser lifecycle","Query blocks and System R overview","Logical equivalences and safe simplifications","Join reorder, cross products, transitive predicates","Selection/projection pushdown heuristics","Physical implementation alternatives"],"limitations":["The official 2×2 slide-layout PDF was used because the one-slide-per-page version was unavailable during source review."]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-15","lecture":15,"title":"Query Optimisation: Plan Space Example","short":"One query, several cost decisions","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Start with a simple plan, then recalculate its cost after changing filters, join orientation, and materialization.","goals":["Recalculate the cost after each plan refinement","Distinguish the effects of selection pushdown on the outer and inner inputs","Compare both choices of outer and inner join input","Include the cost of materializing a filtered inner input"],"sections":[{"id":"l15-s1","title":"An original practice problem","html":"<p><strong>Original practice problem:</strong> Orders occupies 600 pages and Customers occupies 200 pages. A filter keeps 2% of Orders, equivalent to 12 pages after the surviving tuples are packed together. Another filter keeps 25% of Customers, equivalent to 50 pages. There are B=8 buffer frames. The query joins on customer_id and returns only name. Both base tables are heap files, so filtering requires a full scan. Count only page I/O, excluding CPU work and final-output writes. Filtered output is pipelined directly into the join unless materialization is explicitly stated.</p><p>First try a page nested-loop join with Customers as the outer input:</p><ol><li>Without filtering, read the 200 Customer pages and scan all 600 Order pages for each one. The total is 200+200×600=120,200 I/Os.</li><li>Filter Customers first. Reading it still costs 200 I/Os, but its surviving tuples occupy only 50 packed outer pages. Orders is now scanned 50 times, giving 200+50×600=30,200 I/Os.</li><li>Add the Orders filter during each inner scan. Each scan must still read all 600 Order pages. Fewer rows survive the filter, but the cost remains 30,200 I/Os.</li></ol><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Fewer rows after a filter do not necessarily mean fewer pages read.</b> If every inner scan still reads the base table, the 12 pages of filtered Orders output do not replace the 600 pages that must be scanned.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 3–13</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l15-s2","title":"Reverse the inputs, then materialize","html":"<p>Reverse the join and use filtered Orders as the outer input. Scanning Orders costs 600 I/Os and produces 12 outer pages. Each outer page scans the 200-page Customers table, giving 600+12×200=3,000 I/Os.</p><p>Now materialize filtered Customers so that repeated scans can read its smaller temporary result:</p><ol><li>Read Customers for 200 I/Os, then write its 50-page temporary result for another 50 I/Os.</li><li>Scan Orders once for 600 I/Os, retaining 12 packed outer pages after filtering.</li><li>For each outer page, reread the 50 temporary pages. This costs 12×50=600 I/Os.</li></ol><p>The total is 600+200+50+12×50=1,450 I/Os. Alternatively, use Customers as the outer input and materialize filtered Orders. Read both base tables, write 12 temporary pages, then reread those 12 pages for each of the 50 outer pages. The cost is 200+600+12+50×12=1,412 I/Os.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Separate base-table reads, temporary writes, and repeated temporary reads.</b> Materialization reduces repeated-scan costs, but it is not free: the temporary result must also be written.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 14–24</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l15-s3","title":"Change the join algorithm and tuple width","html":"<p>Replace page nested loops with block nested loops. Of the 8 frames, reserve 1 for the inner input and 1 for output, leaving 6 frames for each outer block. Keep filtered Customers as the outer input and materialize the 12-page Orders result as above. The 50 outer pages need ceil(50/6)=9 blocks, each of which reads the 12-page inner result. Read both base tables for 200+600 I/Os, write 12 temporary pages, and reread them 9 times. The total is 200+600+12+ceil(50/6)×12=920 I/Os.</p><p>Projection can reduce memory requirements. If each 48-byte Orders tuple is reduced to its 8-byte customer_id, the 12 filtered pages shrink to approximately 12×8/48=2 pages, ignoring differences in page overhead and packing. Keep duplicate customer_id values: each surviving order must still contribute its matching output row. These 2 pages fit in the 6 outer frames. Scan Orders once and keep its keys in RAM, then scan Customers once to perform the join. No temporary write is needed, so the cost is 600+200=800 I/Os.</p><p>Sort-merge is another candidate, but its passes must be counted for each filtered input. If reading the base table has already been counted, pass 0 adds the run writes without charging the same input read again. Index nested loops counts outer tuples to determine the number of probes; narrower tuples do not by themselves reduce that number. Clustered selection may reduce the base pages read. An inner index is useful only if it supports the join key.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 25–42</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l15-s4","title":"Exercise: check the assumptions","html":"<p>Change the assumptions: filtered Orders contains 1,200 tuples on 12 pages, and clustered access retrieves them for 12 I/Os. Use each tuple to look up a unique key in Customers. The index is already in RAM, and each lookup needs just 1 I/O to read data. What is the total cost? Why might this still not be the best plan?</p><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><p>Read the outer input for 12 I/Os, then perform 1,200 lookups. The total is 12+1,200×1=1,212 I/Os. The plan that retains keys in RAM above costs only 800 I/Os under its stated assumptions. If only 30 outer tuples remain, there are far fewer lookups and the index join may win. The presence of an index is not enough to choose a plan: cardinality, or the number of tuples processed, also matters. This is an original exercise, separate from the Sailors/Reserves example in the PDF.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=39\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 39–43</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Does filtering an inner heap from 600 pages to 12 output pages automatically reduce each scan to 12 I/Os?","options":["Yes, always","No, if each scan still reads the 600-page heap","It depends only on the number of SELECT columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Output size and the number of input pages that must be read are different quantities."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage includes repeated plan refinement; selection pushdown inside and outside nested loops; join orientation; materializing filtered inner input; sort-merge pass accounting; block nested loops and projection width; and clustered outer access with an indexed inner join. The explanations and calculations are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–43"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–13"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=14","label":"PDF pages 14–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=25","label":"PDF pages 25–38"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=39","label":"PDF pages 39–43"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–43","topics":["Repeated plan refinement","Selection pushdown inside/outside nested loops","Join orientation","Materialising filtered inner input","Sort-merge pass accounting","Block nested loops, projection width","Clustered outer lookup and indexed inner join"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-16","lecture":16,"title":"Access Methods","short":"Scans, indexes, and bitmaps","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Determine when a query can use an index, and when a sequential scan or bitmap scan has a lower cost.","goals":["Distinguish sequential-scan cost from output selectivity","Check whether an index can support a predicate in key order","Apply the equality-prefix/range rule to a composite B+ tree","Compare index-only, clustered, and unclustered scans"],"sections":[{"id":"l16-s1","title":"Access paths: ways to execute an operator","html":"<p>An access path is a way of reading data to execute an operator. For example, a selection can use a sequential scan, an index scan, or a combination of several indexes through a bitmap. Selectivity is the fraction of rows retained by a filtering condition.</p><p><strong>Original example:</strong> a table contains 10,000 rows on 100 pages, and a predicate retains 10% of the rows. A heap scan still reads all 100 pages to test the predicate, leaving 1,000 rows. If row width stays the same and the output rows are packed into pages as densely as before, the output occupies approximately 10 pages. Unclustered lookups for the 1,000 matching rows may read many scattered pages; having an index does not guarantee fewer I/Os than a scan.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Distinguish pages read from pages remaining after filtering.</b> Low selectivity produces a small output, but a sequential scan still reads the whole table unless some mechanism lets it skip pages.</p></aside><p>In general, a sequential scan of an input containing N pages costs N page reads. The estimate output pages ≈ selectivity × N applies only when row width and tuple packing remain unchanged.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–6</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l16-s2","title":"Composite B+ trees: an equality prefix followed by a range","html":"<p>An index on (department,age,salary) sorts first by department, then by age, and finally by salary. Searching for department=CS AND age&gt;30 means locating the CS group and reading its portion with age&gt;30, which is a contiguous index range. The condition department=CS AND age=30 AND salary&gt;500 also forms a contiguous range: fix the first two columns, then use a range on the third. A search for age=30 that omits department is not a left-prefix lookup in this teaching model.</p><p>For department=CS AND age&gt;30 AND salary=500, the index locates the CS entries with age&gt;30, after which salary must be checked in the entries found. Entries with salary=500 can occur in several different age groups, so the full predicate does not form one contiguous range.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Read a composite key from left to right.</b> In this B+ tree model, fix the leading columns with equality conditions, then use one range. Conditions on later columns may still require additional checks.</p></aside><p>A hash index on (department,age) hashes the complete pair, so equality on both keys is needed to choose a bucket. Knowing only department, or specifying an age range, is insufficient for that kind of lookup.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 7–15</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l16-s3","title":"Index-only access and multiple indexes","html":"<p>With index-only access, a query needs only attributes already present in the index, so the teaching model can avoid reading the data tuples. When records still have to be fetched, clustering matters: a clustered index places matching records near one another, while an unclustered index may lead to many scattered pages. Sorting RIDs (record identifiers) by location before fetching helps group accesses to records on the same page.</p><p><strong>Original example:</strong> an age index returns RIDs {1,3,6,9}, and a department index returns {2,3,6,8}. An AND condition takes their intersection, {3,6}. An OR condition takes their union, {1,2,3,6,8,9}. A bitmap represents candidate locations and helps organise heap reads by page. After fetching, predicates not satisfied by the indexes must still be checked, along with any required rechecks; not every candidate necessarily becomes an output row.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 16–21</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l16-s4","title":"An additional EXPLAIN exercise","html":"<p>Create a separate test table in PostgreSQL. Run EXPLAIN for predicates with few and many matches, then compare it with EXPLAIN ANALYZE on a safe SELECT. Record estimated rows, actual rows, scan type, and buffer statistics if that option is enabled. ANALYZE actually executes the query; use it with data-changing statements only when you understand and accept their effects.</p><details><summary>What to observe</summary><p>The planner may switch from an index scan to a bitmap scan to a sequential scan as selectivity changes. The official practical uses Bay Area Bike Sharing on Learn; its login-restricted dataset and instructions were not accessed here. This experiment is an original supplementary exercise, not a copy of the Learn practical.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 22–23</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which predicate forms a contiguous range in an index on (A,B)?","options":["A=7 AND B>20","B=20 with any value of A","A>7 AND B=20 with no residual check needed at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An equality prefix on A followed by a range on B forms one contiguous interval in the index."},"source":{"summary":"The complete official PDF was read. Topics covered: Sequential scan and selectivity; Index eligibility and key ordering; Composite B+ tree prefix/range rule; Index-only, clustered, unclustered scans; Hash composite equality; Multi-index RID intersection/union; PostgreSQL EXPLAIN practical scope. The explanations and calculations above are original teaching material, not reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–23"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–7"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=8","label":"PDF pages 8–16"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=17","label":"PDF pages 17–23"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–23","topics":["Sequential scan and selectivity","Index eligibility and key ordering","Composite B+ tree prefix/range rule","Index-only, clustered, unclustered scans","Hash composite equality","Multi-index RID intersection/union","PostgreSQL EXPLAIN practical scope"],"limitations":["The Learn practical and dataset require separate access and were not verified."]},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-17","lecture":17,"title":"Query Optimisation: Costing","short":"Estimate cardinality before cost","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Estimate cardinality, the number of rows, at each step before costing the whole plan.","goals":["Estimate operator costs and the cardinalities passed between steps","Read catalog statistics and explain when they need updating","Identify uniformity and independence assumptions","Calculate selectivity for equality, range, NOT, AND, and OR conditions"],"sections":[{"id":"l17-s1","title":"Wrong cardinalities lead to wrong costs","html":"<p>An optimizer needs to estimate how many rows each step will process before calculating its cost. The output estimate of a child operator becomes the input estimate of its parent, so an error can propagate through the whole plan. The catalog stores statistics such as tuple counts, page counts, numbers of distinct values, minimum and maximum values, and index heights. Statistics are usually approximate and need updating as the data changes. The System R model combines page I/O with the number of tuples processed by the CPU, using a weight to compare the two components.</p><p><strong>Original example:</strong> a table has 12,000 rows and 40 departments. Assuming that all departments have the same number of rows (uniformity), filtering for one department retains 12,000/40=300 rows. If a second condition retains 20% and is independent of the first condition (independence), 300×0.2=60 rows remain. Correlation between the columns can make this multiplication substantially wrong.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Selectivity is the fraction retained, not the fraction discarded.</b> “Highly selective” usually describes a condition that retains very few rows, meaning its selectivity value is small.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–6</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l17-s2","title":"Formulas and their assumptions","html":"<p>For equality A=c, suppose c is in the actual set of values and all values of column A occur equally often. Selecting one value retains one of V(A) equal shares, where V(A) is the number of distinct values of A. The selectivity is therefore 1/V(A). For a range, count the values retained: an integer A from 10 through 29 has 20 possible values. A&gt;24 retains 25,26,27,28,29, giving a fraction of 5/20 under a uniform distribution.</p><p>To combine conditions, let s be the selectivity of P, and let s1 and s2 be the selectivities of P and Q. First assume there are no NULL values, so each condition is either TRUE or FALSE:</p><ul><li>NOT P retains the part discarded by P: 1−s.</li><li>P AND Q: under independence, retain a fraction s2 of the fraction s1, giving s1s2. For example, 0.2×0.3=0.06.</li><li>P OR Q: add the two fractions and subtract their overlap to avoid double counting. Under independence, the overlap is s1s2, so OR has selectivity s1+s2−s1s2. For example, 0.2+0.3−0.06=0.44.</li></ul><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>The multiplication formula requires independence.</b> If the conditions are correlated, their overlap is not s1s2. SQL NULL introduces UNKNOWN, so even the formula for NOT needs to be reconsidered.</p></aside><p><strong>Equijoin example:</strong> R contains 600 rows and 30 distinct join-key values; S contains 900 rows and 60 distinct values. Assume uniform distributions and that all 30 keys in R also occur in S. Each key has 600/30=20 rows in R and 900/60=15 rows in S. Each shared key produces 20×15=300 pairs, so 30 keys produce 30×300=9,000 rows.</p><p>This gives the estimate |R||S|/max(V(R.A),V(S.A)): |R| and |S| are row counts; V(R.A) and V(S.A) are the numbers of distinct values of join key A on each side; max selects the larger number. In this example, 600×900/60=9,000. The formula requires uniformity and the assumption that the smaller key set is contained in the larger one, as in the example. A different amount of overlap requires an adjustment.</p><p>If S.A is a valid, non-NULL foreign key referencing unique key R.A, each row of S matches exactly one row of R, so the join contains exactly |S| rows. If the foreign key permits NULL, those NULL rows do not match in this equijoin and the output count may be lower.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 7–11</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l17-s3","title":"Histograms: whole buckets and partial buckets","html":"<p>A histogram divides the value domain into buckets and records the number of rows in each bucket. <strong>Original example:</strong> two equi-width integer buckets have equal widths: [1,4] contains 20 rows and [5,8] contains 60 rows. To estimate A=6, assume the 4 values in the second bucket occur equally often: 60/4=15 rows. For A&gt;6, retain only the two values 7 and 8: 2/4×60=30 rows.</p><p>Uniformity within a bucket is still an assumption; it does not mean the real data has exactly 15 rows for each value. Equi-width histograms use equally wide value intervals. Equi-depth histograms keep approximately the same number of rows in each bucket, using narrower intervals in denser regions. More buckets describe the distribution in greater detail but occupy more catalog space. When statistics are missing, an optimizer uses defaults that depend on the system and version; these are not measured probabilities.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 13–25</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l17-s4","title":"Exercise: find the independence error","html":"<p>A table contains 10,000 people. Of these, 20% live in city X and 20% have a postcode in region X. In reality, the two conditions match exactly the same group of people. What row count does the independence assumption estimate for AND? What is the actual count?</p><details><summary>View the solution</summary><p>The estimate is 10,000×0.2×0.2=400 rows. In reality, both conditions retain the same 20% group, giving 10,000×0.2=2,000 rows, 5 times the estimate. This error can cause the optimizer to choose an unsuitable join algorithm or memory allocation. A single-column histogram models a distribution better than assuming uniformity across the whole column, but it does not by itself resolve correlation between two columns.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 26–26</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"An equi-width bucket contains 4 values and 60 rows. What is the estimated equality count for one value?","options":["4","15","60"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Assuming uniformity within the bucket gives 60/4=15. This is an estimate based on equal frequencies, not the actual count if the data is skewed."},"source":{"summary":"The complete official PDF was read. Topics covered: Operator costs and propagated cardinalities; Catalog statistics and refresh; Uniformity and independence; Equality/range/NOT/AND/OR selectivity; Key–foreign key and general equijoin size; Missing statistics defaults; Equi-width/equi-depth histogram equality/range estimates. The explanations and calculations above are original teaching material, not reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–12"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pages 13–20"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pages 21–26"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–26","topics":["Operator costs and propagated cardinalities","Catalog statistics and refresh","Uniformity and independence","Equality/range/NOT/AND/OR selectivity","Key–foreign key and general equijoin size","Missing statistics defaults","Equi-width/equi-depth histogram equality/range estimates"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-18","lecture":18,"title":"Query Optimisation: Searching","short":"Dynamic programming and interesting orders","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Use dynamic programming to search for a plan while retaining subplans with useful output orders.","goals":["Enumerate and cost access paths for a single table","Explain why the number of join trees grows rapidly","Distinguish left-deep, bushy, and right-deep join trees","Understand the benefits and limits of cross-product pruning"],"sections":[{"id":"l18-s1","title":"The search starts with a single table","html":"<p>Start with each base table: consider a heap scan and every index path that can support its predicates. Add the cost of testing remaining conditions (residual filtering) or removing duplicates when needed. Combining multiple tables also introduces choices of join order and join algorithm, so the number of candidates grows rapidly. The optimizer must limit its search to avoid taking too long.</p><p><strong>Original example:</strong> a table has 20,000 tuples on 200 data pages, and a suitable index has 20 leaf pages. A predicate retains 5% of the tuples. Ignore root-to-leaf traversal cost to focus on the subsequent reads:</p><ol><li>Assuming an appropriate distribution of index entries, read approximately 5% of the 20 leaf pages: 0.05×20=1 I/O.</li><li>With a clustered index, matching tuples are close together and occupy approximately 5% of the 200 data pages: 0.05×200=10 I/Os. The total is approximately 1+10=11 I/Os.</li><li>With an unclustered index, 0.05×20,000=1,000 tuples must be fetched. In a model where every fetch requires a data-page read, the total is 1+1,000=1,001 I/Os. A page may be read repeatedly; these are not 1,000 distinct pages.</li><li>A heap scan reads all 200 pages, costing 200 I/Os.</li></ol><p>In compact form, the calculations are clustered ≈0.05×(20+200)=11 and unclustered ≈0.05×(20+20,000)=1,001. The clustered estimate counts data pages; the unclustered estimate uses the number of tuples to estimate the number of fetches. Caching or grouping fetches by page can lower unclustered access cost relative to this model.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Even at the same selectivity, clustering can change the best plan.</b> Count index-page reads and data fetches separately. Do not substitute tuple counts for page counts without stating the assumption that justifies it.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–7</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l18-s2","title":"Left-deep trees: one way to limit the plan space","html":"<p>In a left-deep tree, the right input of every join is a base relation, while the left input can be the result of earlier joins. A right-deep tree reverses this arrangement, with base relations on the left. A bushy tree allows both inputs to be intermediate results. Considering only left-deep trees reduces the number of candidates and suits index nested-loop joins or pipelining, but it can exclude a good bushy plan. Blocking operators may still require materialisation.</p><p>In the join graph R—S—T, predicates connect R to S and S to T, but not R to T. The optimizer therefore often discards the candidate {R,T}, which creates a Cartesian product, and instead extends subsets {R,S} and {S,T}. This is a search-space heuristic, not a proof that a Cartesian product is always the slowest choice for every dataset.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 8–12</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l18-s3","title":"Dynamic programming must remember physical properties","html":"<p>Dynamic programming (DP) saves solutions to subproblems so they do not have to be recomputed:</p><ol><li>Pass 1: retain the necessary access plans for each base table.</li><li>Pass 2: add another table to each single-table set, comparing outer/inner choices and join algorithms.</li><li>Continue in the same way: in pass k, add one base relation to retained plans for subsets of k−1 tables. Here, k is the number of tables combined, not the number of data-reading passes.</li></ol><p><strong>Original example:</strong> two plans for {R,S} return the same result. Plan A costs 100 units but is unordered; plan B costs 125 and is already sorted by customer_id. The operator immediately above requires ORDER BY customer_id: adding a sort after A costs 80, while B needs no additional sort, costing 0. The totals are A=100+80=180 and B=125+0=125. Retaining only A because it is cheaper as a subplan would discard the better plan for the whole query.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Retain plans with useful orders as well as the cheapest current plan.</b> An interesting order is an order that may let a later join, GROUP BY, or ORDER BY avoid sorting again.</p></aside><p>DP therefore retains the cheapest overall plan and the cheapest plan for each relevant interesting order. “Best” must be qualified by the output's physical properties, such as the key on which it is already sorted.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 13–23</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l18-s4","title":"Exercise: build a DP table","html":"<p>Suppose the retained plan for RS costs 40 and outputs 10 pages, while the plan for ST costs 30 and outputs 100 pages. Adding T to RS costs another 20; adding R to ST costs another 80. All costs use the same unit. Which plan should you choose? What if the required final order adds 50 to the first plan and 0 to the second?</p><details><summary>View the solution</summary><p>Without a final-order requirement, the RS route costs 40+20=60, less than the ST route's 30+80=110. With the required order, the first plan costs 60+50=110 and the second costs 110+0=110, so they tie. A cheaper subplan alone is not enough to decide: account for the operators above it and the output's physical properties. After choosing a join order, the total cost must still include any GROUP BY, aggregation, or sorting required by the query.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Source topic: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 14–24</a>. The numerical examples and exercises in this section are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Why retain a more expensive subplan that already produces sorted output?","options":["Because sorted output is always better","Because it may avoid an expensive sort later","Because subplan cost does not matter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An interesting order can reduce the total plan cost by avoiding a later sort, even if that subplan costs more."},"source":{"summary":"The complete official PDF was read. Topics covered: Single-table access enumeration and cost; Join-tree combinatorial growth; Left-deep versus bushy/right-deep; Cross-product pruning; Dynamic programming and optimal substructure; Interesting orders and property-aware retention; Final grouping/sorting cost. The explanations and calculations above are original teaching material, not reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–7"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=8","label":"PDF pages 8–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pages 20–24"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–24","topics":["Single-table access enumeration and cost","Join-tree combinatorial growth","Left-deep versus bushy/right-deep","Cross-product pruning","Dynamic programming and optimal substructure","Interesting orders and property-aware retention","Final grouping/sorting cost"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-19","lecture":19,"title":"Transactions: correctness under concurrency","short":"ACID · schedules · conflict graphs","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"A transaction groups several data operations into one unit of work. ACID describes the guarantees on which an application can rely.","goals":["Distinguish the four ACID properties","Construct a precedence graph from a read/write schedule","Distinguish conflict serializability from view serializability"],"sections":[{"id":"l19-s1","title":"The conceptual map","html":"<p>Moving 5 boxes from the North warehouse to the South warehouse requires two writes: subtracting from North and adding to South. We group these operations into a <strong>transaction</strong> so that the application does not have to accept a transfer completed only halfway.</p><ul><li><strong>Atomicity:</strong> commit all changes, or abort and remove their effects.</li><li><strong>Consistency:</strong> a transaction must preserve invariants, such as an unchanged total number of boxes. The application still has to implement its business rules correctly.</li><li><strong>Isolation:</strong> in the serializability model studied here, transactions may interleave, but their effects must be equivalent to some serial execution order.</li><li><strong>Durability:</strong> committed results survive the kinds of failures that the system promises to handle.</li></ul><p>The DBMS manages database reads and writes. Shipping a physical item does not automatically reverse when ROLLBACK occurs. Identify the transaction boundary before choosing a mechanism.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 1–17</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l19-s2","title":"Worked example: two warehouses","html":"<p>North has 40 boxes and South has 60. T1 moves 5 boxes from North → South, while T2 checks the total. In either serial schedule, with T1 completing before T2 or the reverse, T2 sees 100. If T2 reads North after the subtraction and South before the addition, it instead sees 35 + 60 = 95.</p><ol><li>Before the transfer, the quantities are (40,60).</li><li>T1 writes North = 35.</li><li>T2 reads North = 35 and South = 60.</li><li>T1 writes South = 65.</li></ol><p>The final database state is correct, but the answer already returned is wrong. Checking only the final state is insufficient; the values read during execution also matter.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 18–26</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l19-s3","title":"Build a conflict graph","html":"<p>Read the schedule <code>R1(X), W1(X), R2(X), W2(Y), R1(Y)</code> from left to right. R1(X) means that T1 reads X, and W2(Y) means that T2 writes Y. A schedule preserves the operation order within each transaction.</p><ol><li>Create one vertex for each transaction: T1 and T2.</li><li>On X, W1 precedes R2, so add T1 → T2.</li><li>On Y, W2 precedes R1, so add T2 → T1.</li><li>The two edges form a cycle: no serial order can place T1 both before and after T2.</li></ol><p>Add an edge only when the two operations belong to different transactions, access the same object, and include at least one write. This structure is called a conflict graph or precedence graph.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A cycle rules out conflict serializability.</b> Each edge imposes an order between two transactions, and a cycle makes those ordering requirements contradictory.</p></aside><p>If the final pair becomes <code>R1(Y), W2(Y)</code>, every edge points T1 → T2, matching the serial order T1 then T2. Two reads do not create an edge, and neither do operations on different objects.</p><p><strong>Check your understanding:</strong> Would adding R3(Z) at the start create a cycle? No, because its access to Z conflicts with no other operation.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 27–42</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l19-s4","title":"Recognize three anomalies","html":"<p>The initial price is 100. T1 writes a price of 80 but <strong>has not committed</strong>. T2 then reads 80. This is already a <strong>dirty read</strong>, because T2 has read an uncommitted value written by another transaction.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A dirty read is identified at the moment of the read.</b> If T1 later commits, the earlier read is still dirty. If T1 aborts, the price returns to 100, but T2 may already have used 80 in its calculation.</p></aside><ul><li><strong>Unrepeatable read:</strong> a transaction reads the same price twice and sees different values because another transaction updates and commits between the reads.</li><li><strong>Dirty read:</strong> a transaction reads another transaction's uncommitted value, regardless of whether the writer eventually commits or aborts.</li><li><strong>Lost update:</strong> two transactions update from the same old value, and a later write overwrites the effect of an earlier update.</li></ul><p>Exercise: 8 seats remain. Two customers both read 8 and each writes 7. Two successful bookings should leave 6 seats; a result of 7 is a lost update. Draw the R–W edges to find the cycle.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF page 29 · slide 32 (dirty read); pages 28–30 (anomalies)</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l19-s5","title":"View equivalence and its limits","html":"<p>View equivalence preserves the source of every read: whether it reads an initial value or a value written by a particular transaction. It also preserves the final writer of each object. Consider <code>W1(X), W2(X), W1(X)</code>. Its graph has edges in both directions, but it has no reads and T1 is the final writer. It is view-equivalent to T2 followed by T1.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>An acyclic graph characterizes conflict serializability.</b> A cycle alone does not disprove view serializability: the blind-write example here still preserves every read source and final writer.</p></aside><p>This example explains why conflict serializability is the stricter condition. A cyclic schedule does not necessarily fail every definition of equivalence; the precise conclusion is that it fails the conflict criterion. Real applications also need abort handling and durability, beyond the graph test.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=43\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 43–48</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Is R1(A), W2(A), W1(B), R2(B) conflict-serializable?","options":["Yes, in the order T1 then T2","Yes, in the order T2 then T1","No, because the graph has a cycle","There is insufficient information because two objects are involved"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both R1(A) before W2(A) and W1(B) before R2(B) create T1 → T2. There is no edge in the reverse direction."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 19 introduces transactions, ACID, execution schedules, conflicts, and two serializability criteria. A dependency graph checks conflict serializability, while logging or shadow paging supports atomicity and durability.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–17"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=27","label":"PDF pages 27–42"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=29","label":"PDF page 29 · slide 32 (dirty read); pages 28–30 (anomalies)"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=43","label":"PDF pages 43–48"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–48","topics":["ACID","transaction abstraction","schedules","RW/WR/WW anomalies","conflict equivalence","precedence graph","view equivalence","durability"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"transactions"},{"id":"lecture-20","lecture":20,"title":"Locking: from locks to correct schedules","short":"S/X locks · 2PL · deadlock","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"A lock controls access to a data object. A locking protocol specifies when a transaction may acquire and release its locks.","goals":["Simulate shared/exclusive locks and the two locking phases","Explain why cascading aborts occur","Handle deadlocks through detection or timestamp-based prevention"],"sections":[{"id":"l20-s1","title":"The essential rules","html":"<p>Two people viewing an order can read it concurrently. When one updates the order, other transactions must be prevented from reading or writing the data protected by that update lock. Locks provide this access control.</p><p>An <strong>S-lock</strong>, or shared lock, permits reading. Multiple transactions may hold S-locks on the same object. An <strong>X-lock</strong>, or exclusive lock, permits writing and conflicts with other transactions' S-locks and X-locks.</p><p><strong>2PL</strong> has two phases. In the growing phase, a transaction may acquire or upgrade locks. Its first lock release begins the shrinking phase, during which it may only release locks. It therefore cannot acquire or upgrade a lock after releasing one.</p><p>In this lecture, <strong>strict 2PL</strong> retains all locks until the transaction ends. Some sources call retention of both S-locks and X-locks rigorous 2PL, so state the convention you are using.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>The first lock release is the phase boundary in 2PL.</b> A transaction cannot return to acquiring locks afterward. The lecture's strict variant additionally delays every release until transaction completion.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–14</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l20-s2","title":"Worked example: updating an order","html":"<p>T1 needs to update Order and Stock. The sequence <code>X(Order), update Order, unlock(Order), X(Stock)</code> violates 2PL at its final request. Replacing it with <code>X(Order), X(Stock), update both, commit, unlock</code> satisfies the lecture's strict variant.</p><p>T2, which only reads Order, must wait while T1 holds X(Order). If T1 aborts, T2 has never read the uncommitted version, so T2's result need not be withdrawn. This operational benefit goes beyond merely proving that a precedence graph is acyclic.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 3–17</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l20-s3","title":"Deadlock and conflict cycles","html":"<p>Suppose T1 holds X(Order) and T2 holds X(Stock). T1 requests Stock, while T2 requests Order. The waits-for graph contains T1 → T2 → T1. T1 cannot proceed until T2 releases Stock, and T2 cannot proceed until T1 releases Order.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Waits-for graphs and precedence graphs answer different questions.</b> A waits-for graph records who is waiting for whom to detect deadlock. A precedence graph records the order of conflicting operations to check conflict serializability.</p></aside><p>Aborting T2, undoing its changes, and releasing Stock lets T1 proceed. Victim selection should consider work already performed and previous restarts to avoid starvation. Preclaiming all required locks as one all-or-nothing request at the start avoids holding some locks while waiting for others, but it requires knowing the lock set in advance. Merely requesting locks one by one at the start does not provide this guarantee.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 18–22</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l20-s4","title":"Reasoning with timestamps","html":"<p>T_old has timestamp 10 and T_new has timestamp 30. A smaller timestamp means that the transaction started earlier.</p><ul><li>Wait-die: if old requests a lock held by new, old waits; if new requests a lock held by old, new aborts.</li><li>Wound-wait: if old requests a lock held by new, new is forced to abort; if new requests a lock held by old, new waits.</li></ul><p>Each rule permits waiting edges in only one timestamp direction. A waiting chain therefore cannot loop back to its starting transaction. A restarted transaction retains its original timestamp so it is not repeatedly treated as the newest transaction.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=23\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 23–27</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l20-s5","title":"Check your understanding","html":"<p>T1 acquires S(A), acquires S(B), releases S(A), and then upgrades S(B) to X(B). Is this valid? No. An upgrade after the shrinking phase has begun also violates 2PL.</p><p>Next question: does strict 2PL eliminate deadlock? No. Construct a counterexample with two transactions acquiring A and B in opposite orders. Serializability is a safety property; avoiding indefinite waits is a separate progress requirement.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 7–28</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"T_old requests a lock held by T_new. What does wound-wait do?","options":["T_old aborts","T_new aborts and releases its locks","T_old always waits","Both transactions receive the X-lock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under wound-wait, a transaction with a smaller timestamp can force a younger transaction holding the requested lock to abort."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 20 covers shared and exclusive locks, 2PL, strict 2PL, deadlock detection, and the wait-die and wound-wait prevention rules.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–17"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–22"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=23","label":"PDF pages 23–27"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=7","label":"PDF pages 7–28"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–28","topics":["lock compatibility","growing/shrinking phases","strict 2PL","cascading aborts","waits-for graph","victim selection","wait-die","wound-wait"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"transactions"},{"id":"lecture-21","lecture":21,"title":"Recovery: why the log must come first","short":"STEAL/FORCE · WAL · ARIES","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"After a crash, disk pages may both lack committed updates and contain uncommitted ones. Recovery must handle both possibilities.","goals":["Derive UNDO and REDO requirements from the buffer policy","Apply the two mandatory WAL ordering rules","Follow the analysis, redo, and undo phases"],"sections":[{"id":"l21-s1","title":"Buffer policies: STEAL and FORCE","html":"<p>Suppose an uncommitted update reaches disk before the machine crashes. On restart, the DBMS must remove it using <strong>UNDO</strong>. Conversely, if a committed update exists only in RAM when the machine fails, the DBMS must reconstruct it using <strong>REDO</strong>.</p><p>To <strong>flush</strong> a page means to write it from RAM to durable storage. A <strong>STEAL</strong> policy permits flushing pages containing uncommitted updates, so UNDO may be needed. <strong>NO-FORCE</strong> permits committing before every modified data page is flushed, so REDO may be needed. STEAL + NO-FORCE therefore requires support for both.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>STEAL requires UNDO support; NO-FORCE requires REDO support.</b> These requirements arise because a data page may reach disk at a different time from its transaction's commit.</p></aside><p>NO-STEAL + FORCE simplifies some reasoning, but it requires retaining uncommitted pages in RAM and flushing many pages at commit. A mechanism is still needed for crashes during a multi-page flush: the two policy labels do not make a sequence of writes atomic.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 2–17</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l21-s2","title":"Worked example: two products","html":"<p>Disk contains A = 12 and B = 7. T1 changes A to 10 and commits; T2 changes B to 3 without committing. Suppose B's page has been flushed, A's has not, and the machine crashes.</p><ol><li>After the crash, disk contains A = 12 and B = 3.</li><li>The durable log contains T1: A 12→10, COMMIT; and T2: B 7→3.</li><li>Restoring T1 requires REDO to set A = 10.</li><li>Removing T2's effects requires UNDO to restore B = 7.</li></ol><p>The desired result is A = 10 and B = 7. Both directions are necessary; merely replaying every log record would retain unfinished work.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 18–24</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l21-s3","title":"The two WAL ordering rules","html":"<p>Suppose T1 changes A from 12 to 10. Before the new page A reaches disk, the DBMS must durably store the log information needed to recover that update. Reversing the order creates a crash window in which disk contains 10 but the information needed to restore 12 is missing.</p><ol><li>Update the page in RAM and create a log record in RAM.</li><li>Flush the log records needed for recovery to durable storage.</li><li>Only then may the corresponding data page be flushed.</li><li>At commit, flush the transaction's log through its COMMIT record before reporting success.</li></ol><p>These are the two rules of <strong>WAL, or write-ahead logging</strong>: the log precedes the data page, and the log through COMMIT precedes the successful-commit acknowledgement. Depending on the buffer policy, data-page flushing may occur before or after commit.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>WAL does not require every data page to be on disk at commit.</b> Under NO-FORCE, a durable log provides the information needed for REDO after a crash. A COMMIT record present only in RAM is insufficient for reporting success.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 18–24</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l21-s4","title":"ARIES: repeat history, then undo","html":"<p>In the A/B example, committed T1 is a <strong>winner</strong>, while uncommitted T2 is a <strong>loser</strong>. ARIES handles the crash in three phases:</p><ol><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> identify transactions and dirty pages that may need recovery.</li><li><strong>Redo:</strong> reconstruct history up to the crash, including loser updates when needed.</li><li><strong>Undo:</strong> remove loser effects so unfinished work is not retained.</li></ol><p>Do not conclude that ARIES redo considers only T1. “Repeat history, then undo” describes the algorithm's sequence; its final result must still retain T1 and remove T2's effects. A complete implementation also needs pageLSN values, compensation log records, and detailed checkpoint rules.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 25–27</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l21-s5","title":"Failure models and design questions","html":"<p>If power fails but storage remains intact, the durable log supports recovery. If both the data and log are destroyed, this reasoning is insufficient: a replica or backup in another failure domain is needed.</p><p>Check your understanding: which property is endangered by reporting commit success before flushing the log? Durability, because the user has seen success even though a crash could erase the transaction's only surviving record.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 3–5</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"What does STEAL + NO-FORCE typically require?","options":["REDO only","UNDO only","Both UNDO and REDO","No log is needed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"STEAL can put uncommitted data on disk, while NO-FORCE can leave committed data only in RAM."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 21 connects buffer policies to recovery, explains WAL, and introduces the three ARIES phases: analysis, redo, and undo.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–17"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=25","label":"PDF pages 25–27"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–5"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–27","topics":["failure types","buffer policies","UNDO/REDO","logging","WAL ordering","commit durability","ARIES phases"],"limitations":["The lecture introduces ARIES at a high level; it is not a complete implementation specification."]},"lab":"recovery"},{"id":"lecture-22","lecture":22,"title":"Distributed transactions: reaching one decision","short":"Distributed locks · 2PC · recovery","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"Machines participating in one transaction must agree on its outcome, even when messages are delayed and processes restart.","goals":["Detect global deadlock by combining local graphs","Simulate prepare, vote, decision, and acknowledgement","Explain why a prepared participant can become blocked"],"sections":[{"id":"l22-s1","title":"Two global problems","html":"<p>Locks can be managed where their data resides. However, acyclic local waits-for graphs do not prove that the entire system is deadlock-free: combining their edges may reveal a cycle. Commit also requires a common decision rather than independent choices by each machine.</p><p>For example, N1 sees T1 → T2, N2 sees T2 → T3, and N3 sees T3 → T1. Each node sees just one edge, but their union is cyclic. Detecting this requires global information or an equivalent distributed detection protocol.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 3–20</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l22-s2","title":"Worked example: moving stock between regions","html":"<p>East subtracts 4 crates while West adds 4. If East commits and West aborts, the total is incorrect. Coordinator C must therefore establish one decision for participants E/W.</p><ol><li><strong>Prepare:</strong> C asks whether E/W are ready to commit.</li><li><strong>Vote:</strong> each participant checks its conditions. To vote YES, it first durably records PREPARE and the necessary recovery information. Otherwise, it votes NO.</li><li><strong>Decide:</strong> C may choose COMMIT only after receiving every YES; one NO requires ABORT. C durably records its decision before announcing it.</li><li><strong>Finish:</strong> E/W record and implement the final decision, then send ACK.</li></ol><p>E might vote NO because it lacks enough stock. Once E promises YES, it must be able to follow the final decision even after restarting. This is why its PREPARE log must be durable before the vote is sent.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>2PC coordinates commit; 2PL controls concurrent reads and writes.</b> Two-phase commit protects atomic commit across machines. It does not replace an isolation protocol such as two-phase locking.</p></aside><p>Both names contain “two-phase,” but the phases have different meanings: prepare/decision in 2PC, and growing/shrinking lock phases in 2PL.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 21–31</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l22-s3","title":"Crash checklist: which log records matter?","html":"<p>Consider four failure points in the example:</p><ul><li>E loses contact before preparing: it may abort locally.</li><li>E has logged PREPARE and sent YES: it cannot change its mind merely because of a timeout.</li><li>C has logged COMMIT but not yet sent it to W: after recovery, C resends the decision.</li><li>W has committed but its ACK is lost: W sends ACK again without subtracting or adding the stock a second time.</li></ul><p>Repeated messages must be handled according to transaction state. Sequence numbers and transaction IDs distinguish retransmissions from new work.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 32–54</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l22-s4","title":"Why can a prepared participant not simply abort?","html":"<p>E sees only PREPARE in its log. It cannot yet distinguish two situations: C has not decided, or C has already committed and W has applied the decision. If E independently aborts in the second situation, the regions disagree permanently. To preserve atomicity, E must obtain the decision from C or a participant that knows it. If sufficiently reliable information is unavailable, E must wait.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A timeout does not prove that the transaction aborted.</b> Another machine may already have committed while its notification was lost. A prepared participant cannot independently abort merely to stop waiting.</p></aside><p>If E holds locks, this wait also blocks other transactions. A protocol can therefore preserve atomicity while reducing availability. A timeout means only that information has not arrived in time; it does not establish that the other party has not committed.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=47\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 47–57</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"},{"id":"l22-s5","title":"Exercise: combining 2PL and 2PC","html":"<p>T1 has updated stock at E/W and entered PREPARE. T2 wants to read the same item at E. Under strict 2PL, T2 must still wait for T1's final decision. Draw two timelines: one for lock retention and one for commit messages. They meet at transaction completion.</p><p>Check your understanding: why does adding replicas not by itself prove that the protocol is nonblocking? You must also specify which replica has authority to decide and how that decision is recorded or agreed upon. Protocols such as Paxos Commit are mentioned as extensions, without a full treatment in this lecture.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=55\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 55–57</a>. The examples and worked solutions on this lesson page were written independently.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"A participant has only a durable PREPARE record and loses contact with the coordinator. What is safe?","options":["Commit independently because it voted YES","Abort immediately when a timeout expires","Seek the decision; it may have to wait","Delete the log and start over"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A YES vote is a promise to follow the common decision. The coordinator may already have committed elsewhere."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 22 studies partitioned, nonreplicated data, distributed locking, global deadlock, two-phase commit, and the coordination of logging with recovery. Two-phase commit can block progress when the coordinator is unreachable.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–20"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pages 21–31"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=32","label":"PDF pages 32–54"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=47","label":"PDF pages 47–57"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=55","label":"PDF pages 55–57"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–57","topics":["partitioned locks","global deadlock","2PC unanimity","prepare logging","decision logging","recovery cases","strict 2PL integration","blocking"],"limitations":["The lecture assumes partitioning without replication. Consensus-based improvements are mentioned but not developed in detail."]},"lab":"distributed"},{"id":"lecture-23","lecture":23,"title":"Conjunctive Queries: A Query Describes a Pattern","short":"Syntax · semantics · homomorphism","group":"07 · Conjunctive queries","time":25,"lead":"Evaluating a conjunctive query means finding assignments to its variables that make every relational condition true.","goals":["Translate SQL or relational algebra into a conjunctive query","Distinguish output variables from existentially quantified variables","Find homomorphisms and determine the result set"],"sections":[{"id":"l23-s1","title":"A small language with precise meaning","html":"<p>Suppose the database contains R(An,DB) and S(DB). We can match An with DB through R, then check that DB also appears in S, so we return An. The query describing this pattern is <code>Q(x) :- R(x,y), S(y)</code>.</p><p>Read the notation from right to left: find a value of y that makes both R(x,y) and S(y) true, then return x. The <strong>head</strong> is Q(x), which contains the output variables. The <strong>body</strong> is the part after <code>:-</code>. Each relational condition, such as R(x,y), is an <strong>atom</strong>.</p><p>Conjunctive queries correspond to equality selection, projection, and joins without negation, under <strong>set semantics</strong>: the result does not retain duplicate rows. When comparing this mathematical model with SQL, use SELECT DISTINCT and avoid NULL in these examples.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 1–19</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l23-s2","title":"Worked example: who takes a course taught by Linh?","html":"<p>The database contains Enrol(An,DB), Enrol(Bao,AI), Enrol(Chi,DB), Teach(Linh,DB), and Teach(Minh,AI).</p><p>We ask <code>Q(student) :- Enrol(student,course), Teach(Linh,course)</code>.</p><ol><li>The atom Teach(Linh,course) requires course = DB, because that is the course taught by Linh in this database.</li><li>We now look for Enrol(student,DB). It matches with student = An and with student = Chi.</li><li>Returning the student value from each match gives the result {An, Chi}.</li></ol><p>The corresponding SQL is <code>SELECT DISTINCT e.student FROM Enrol e JOIN Teach t ON e.course=t.course WHERE t.teacher=&apos;Linh&apos;</code>. The variable course is existentially quantified: its value is needed to establish a match, but it does not appear in the output.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 20–25</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l23-s3","title":"A homomorphism uses one consistent assignment","html":"<p>Try the assignment h = {student ↦ An, course ↦ DB}. The two atoms become Enrol(An,DB) and Teach(Linh,DB). Both facts occur in the database, so this is a match.</p><p>Now try student ↦ Bao and course ↦ AI. The Enrol atom is satisfied, but Teach(Linh,AI) is absent, so the assignment fails. We cannot replace Linh with Minh, because constants must stay unchanged. We also cannot assign course = AI in the first atom and course = DB in the second. A variable must have one value throughout the same assignment.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A homomorphism must use a single consistent assignment across the entire body.</b> Preserve constants and every occurrence of each variable. Checking atoms separately with incompatible assignments does not establish a match.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 26–31</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l23-s4","title":"Repeated variables and multiple witnesses","html":"<p>The relation Pair contains (a,a), (a,b), and (b,b). The query <code>Q(x) :- Pair(x,x)</code> returns {a,b}. Using x in both positions requires the two values to be equal, so (a,b) does not satisfy the atom.</p><p>For <code>Q(x) :- Pair(x,y)</code>, the output value a has two witnesses: two values of y satisfy the body. Nevertheless, a appears only once in the result set. A homomorphism need not be injective: two different variables may map to the same value.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Multiple witnesses do not duplicate an answer under set semantics.</b> A witness is an assignment showing that the body is true. The result retains only the tuple of head values and removes duplicate tuples.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 26–33</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l23-s5","title":"Practice and worked solution","html":"<p>Suppose the database contains Link(a,b), Link(b,c), and Link(a,c). Find the result of <code>Q(x,z) :- Link(x,y), Link(y,z)</code>.</p><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><p>The only output tuple is (a,c), using y=b. The direct edge Link(a,c) is not enough on its own, because the query requires two consecutive edges. If we add Link(c,c), there will be more witnesses and new output pairs. Enumerate them by keeping the same value of y in both atoms.</p></details><p>A Boolean conjunctive query has no output variables in its head. For example, <code>Q() :- Link(x,y), Link(y,z)</code> asks only whether a two-step path exists.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 19–33</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"In Q(x) :- R(x,y), S(y), what is y?","options":["A constant","An output variable","An existentially quantified variable shared by the two atoms","Two independent variables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The variable y does not appear in the head, but every occurrence of y must receive the same value within an assignment."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 23 connects relational algebra and calculus with conjunctive queries. It defines head and body syntax, homomorphisms, and result semantics in terms of matches.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pages 20–25"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=26","label":"PDF pages 26–31"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=26","label":"PDF pages 26–33"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=19","label":"PDF pages 19–33"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–33","topics":["RA recap","relational calculus","Codd theorem context","CQ syntax","distinguished variables","homomorphism","constants","set semantics"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-24","lecture":24,"title":"Conjunctive Query Evaluation and Static Analysis","short":"Complexity · containment · minimization","group":"07 · Conjunctive queries","time":25,"lead":"Evaluating a query on one database is different from proving that two queries are equivalent on every database.","goals":["Distinguish data complexity from combined complexity","Use the correct homomorphism direction when checking containment","Remove redundant atoms while preserving the output variables"],"sections":[{"id":"l24-s1","title":"Two ways to measure difficulty","html":"<p>Keep a query about shoppers fixed, then increase the database from 100 to 200 values. This measures <strong>data complexity</strong>. If we also allow the query to become longer and contain more variables and atoms, we measure <strong>combined complexity</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>membership</strong> problem considered here is: given a database D, a query Q, and a candidate tuple a, does a belong to Q(D)? We decide YES or NO for one tuple. We are not measuring the time needed to print the entire result set.</p><p>For a fixed query, the number of variables is constant, so a brute-force algorithm runs in polynomial time in the size of the data. When both the query and the data are inputs, general conjunctive-query membership is NP-complete. The next section distinguishes the brute-force count from the proof of that complexity result.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 10–18</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l24-s2","title":"Worked example: count assignments and verify a witness","html":"<p>Try assigning values to three variables x, y, and z. If each variable has 100 candidate values, there are at most 100 × 100 × 100 = 1,000,000 assignments. For each assignment, we must still check the atoms and the tuple produced by the head.</p><p>Let <strong>n</strong> be the number of candidate values and <strong>k</strong> the number of query variables. The brute-force algorithm tries at most <strong>n<sup>k</sup></strong> assignments. If the query is fixed with k = 3, this is n<sup>3</sup>: increasing n from 100 to 200 raises the count from 1,000,000 to 8,000,000. If the query can also change, k is no longer constant. With k = 10 and n = 100, there are 100<sup>10</sup> possible assignments.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>The expression n<sup>k</sup> describes an algorithm; it does not prove NP-completeness.</b> An expensive brute-force search does not rule out a better algorithm. Proving NP-completeness requires both membership in NP and NP-hardness.</p></aside><ol><li><strong>Membership in NP:</strong> use one assignment as a witness. Preserve the constants, substitute the variables, and check that every resulting atom is in D and that the head produces the candidate tuple. The witness size and verification time are polynomial in the input size. Verifying one witness does not require trying every assignment.</li><li><strong>NP-hardness:</strong> reduce 3-coloring to Boolean conjunctive-query evaluation. Create one variable for each graph vertex and an atom Diff(u,v) for each edge (u,v). The database relation Diff contains the six ordered pairs of distinct colors among three colors, such as (red,blue), and contains no equal-color pair. A match exists exactly when the graph can be colored with three colors.</li></ol><p>The reduction creates only one variable per vertex and one atom per edge, so its size is polynomial. A polynomial-time algorithm for every conjunctive-query membership instance under combined complexity would therefore give one for every 3-coloring instance. These two arguments establish NP-completeness. They do not mean that every practical query is slow.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 10–18</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l24-s3","title":"Containment: check the direction of the mapping","html":"<p>Everyone who buys a premium product is also a shopper. Write <code>Qsmall(x) :- Buy(x,p), Premium(p)</code> and <code>Qall(x) :- Buy(x,p)</code>. On every database, the result of Qsmall is contained in the result of Qall: <strong>Qsmall ⊆ Qall</strong>.</p><ol><li>Start with the body of Qall, which contains Buy(x,p).</li><li>Map x ↦ x and p ↦ p into Qsmall. The atom Buy(x,p) is present there, and the head variable x is preserved in its output position.</li><li>Every match of Qsmall already satisfies both Buy and Premium. Reusing its Buy assignment gives a match of Qall with the same output x.</li></ol><p>The homomorphism proving containment goes from <strong>Qall into Qsmall</strong>. More generally, Q1 ⊆ Q2 exactly when there is a homomorphism from Q2 into Q1 that preserves constants and maps the head of Q2 to the head of Q1. This theorem applies to the conjunctive-query set-semantics model considered here, without additional database constraints.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>The homomorphism arrow points in the opposite direction to containment.</b> To show that every result of Q1 also satisfies Q2, find all the conditions of Q2 inside Q1.</p></aside><p>Containment in the other direction fails. A database containing only Buy(An,pen), with no Premium facts, makes Qall return An while Qsmall is empty. Correspondingly, the Premium atom in Qsmall cannot be mapped into the body of Qall.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 19–29</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l24-s4","title":"Canonical databases explain the proof","html":"<p>For <code>Q(x) :- R(x,y), S(y)</code>, construct the small database <code>D = {R(cx,cy), S(cy)}</code> by replacing x with cx and y with cy. Use a distinct fresh constant for each variable, while preserving any constants already in the query. This is the <strong>canonical database</strong> of Q, and cx certainly belongs to Q(D).</p><p>To test Q1 ⊆ Q2, construct the canonical database of Q1 and ask whether Q2 returns the head tuple of Q1 on this database. If it does not, that database is a counterexample to containment. If it does, the match of Q2 gives a homomorphism Q2 → Q1. Composing this mapping with any match of Q1 produces a match of Q2 with the same output.</p><p>The construction also shows that a pure positive conjunctive query of this form is satisfiable: some database gives it a result. This argument assumes there are no external constraints or contradictory additional conditions, such as comparisons that cannot all hold.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=23\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 23–31</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l24-s5","title":"Minimization through a worked example","html":"<p>The query <code>Q(x) :- R(x,y), R(x,z), S(x)</code> is equivalent to <code>Q(x) :- R(x,y), S(x)</code>. Mapping z ↦ y shows that the second R atom adds no requirement. The variables y and z are not required to have different values.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Remove an atom only when the result is preserved on every database.</b> Different variable names may use the same value. However, a variable in the head or an inequality condition can make a proposed simplification invalid.</p></aside><p>If we change the head to Q(x,y,z), we cannot simply remove R(x,z), because all three output values must be preserved and z would otherwise lose its binding. Adding y ≠ z also moves beyond the pure conjunctive queries considered here, and the simplification above is no longer valid.</p><details><summary>Self-check: can S(x) be removed?</summary><p>No. On D = {R(a,b)}, the query after removal returns a, while the original query returns nothing. This single counterexample is enough to disprove equivalence.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 32–37</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"To prove Q1 ⊆ Q2 using the homomorphism theorem, which mapping should you seek?","options":["Q1 → Q2","Q2 → Q1","Database → Q1","Only compare the number of atoms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The mapping goes against the direction of containment and must preserve the head and the constants."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 24 studies the complexity of conjunctive-query evaluation, canonical databases, satisfiability, containment and equivalence through homomorphisms, and minimization to a core.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pages 10–18"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pages 10–18"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=19","label":"PDF pages 19–29"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=23","label":"PDF pages 23–31"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=32","label":"PDF pages 32–37"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–37","topics":["combined complexity","data complexity","NP witness","3-colorability reduction","canonical database","satisfiability","homomorphism theorem","containment/equivalence","minimization","core"],"limitations":["These theoretical results apply to conjunctive queries under set semantics. They do not automatically apply to SQL bags, NULL values, or additional constraints."]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-25","lecture":25,"title":"Acyclic Conjunctive Queries: Use the Tree Structure","short":"Hypergraph · GYO · semijoin","group":"07 · Conjunctive queries","time":25,"lead":"A query can be large yet easier to evaluate because the relationships between its variables have a useful structure.","goals":["Build a hypergraph from the query atoms","Check the running-intersection property and apply GYO reduction","Use bottom-up semijoins to answer a Boolean conjunctive query"],"sections":[{"id":"l25-s1","title":"From atoms to hyperedges","html":"<p>Consider the atoms R(x,y), S(y,z), and T(z,w). Draw one node for each atom and connect them as R—S—T. The nodes containing y are adjacent, and so are the nodes containing z. We want this connectedness property to hold for every variable.</p><p>A <strong>join tree</strong> connects the atoms in a tree such that the nodes containing any given variable form a connected subtree. In other words, if a variable appears at two nodes, it must appear at every node on the path between them. This is the <strong>running-intersection property</strong>.</p><p>In the query hypergraph, each variable is a vertex, and the set of variables in each atom is a <strong>hyperedge</strong>. Do not replace a three-variable hyperedge with three two-variable edges: that substitution can change whether the structure is acyclic. Bounded treewidth and hypertree width are more general structural approaches introduced in the lecture.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 1–13</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l25-s2","title":"Worked example: a chain and a triangle","html":"<p>In this example, a, b, c, and d are all variables. The query <code>Q() :- R(a,b), S(b,c), T(c,d)</code> has the join tree R—S—T. The nodes containing b are the adjacent R and S nodes; those containing c are the adjacent S and T nodes. Each of a and d occurs at only one node.</p><p>Replace T(c,d) with T(c,a), giving the triangle {a,b}, {b,c}, {c,a}. Every tree connecting the three atoms leaves one variable at both ends of a path while it is absent from the middle node. Therefore, no join tree exists. If we add U(a,b,c), we can instead place U at the center and connect each of the other three atoms to it. The running-intersection property then holds, so the new hypergraph is acyclic.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Check the path of each variable in the proposed join tree.</b> Connecting all the atoms in a tree is not enough. A variable cannot appear at both ends of a path and disappear at an intermediate node.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 4–13</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l25-s3","title":"Apply GYO reduction by hand","html":"<p>Start with the chain {a,b}, {b,c}, {c,d}. Remove a and d because each belongs to only one edge. The remaining edges are {b}, {b,c}, and {c}. Remove the two contained edges {b} and {c}, leaving only {b,c}. Now remove b and c, followed by the empty edge. The entire hypergraph has disappeared, so it is acyclic.</p><p>For the triangle with two-variable edges, every variable belongs to two edges, and no edge is contained in another. Reduction gets stuck with a nonempty structure, so the hypergraph is cyclic. The reduction steps may be applied in different orders. Reducing the whole hypergraph to nothing establishes that the query is acyclic.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 14–26</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l25-s4","title":"Yannakakis: remove tuples that cannot contribute to a result","html":"<p>Consider R(A,B)={(u,1),(v,2)}, S(B,C)={(1,k),(2,m)}, and T(C)={k}. The Boolean query asks whether R(A,B), S(B,C), and T(C) can all hold under one assignment.</p><p>A <strong>semijoin</strong> keeps left-hand tuples that match at least one right-hand tuple and returns only the left-hand columns. Thus S ⋉ T uses T to filter S.</p><ol><li>Choose R as the root of the join tree R—S—T.</li><li>Compute S ⋉ T. It keeps (1,k) and removes (2,m), because only k appears in T.</li><li>Compute R ⋉ S using the filtered S. It keeps (u,1) and removes (v,2).</li><li>A tuple remains at the root, so the answer is YES. One witness is the assignment A=u, B=1, C=k.</li></ol><p>In this pass from the leaves to the root, each semijoin checks whether a tuple can be extended into a complete match in the subtree below it. For a Boolean query with a valid join tree, a nonempty root after the bottom-up pass is enough to answer YES.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 27–36</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"},{"id":"l25-s5","title":"Why the tree structure matters","html":"<p>In a join tree, branches can share variables only through the paths connecting them. Therefore, the information that a tuple can be matched down each branch can be combined into one assignment satisfying the whole query. In a cycle, locally valid choices may turn out to be incompatible when the cycle closes.</p><p>Check the example by replacing T={k} with T={q}. The semijoin first makes S empty and then makes R empty, so the answer becomes NO. For a query that returns output tuples, do not claim that every answer can be printed in less time than the output size. The O(|D|·|Q|) bound, where |D| is the database size and |Q| is the query size, is used here for the decision or membership problem and the Boolean algorithm presented.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Returning YES or NO is different from enumerating every answer.</b> A query may produce many output tuples. Printing them alone requires time proportional to the output size.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Related source material: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 28–37</a>. The examples and solutions in this lesson are original supplementary material.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"GYO reduction stops with the triangle {a,b}, {b,c}, {c,a}. What follows?","options":["It is acyclic because it has three edges","It is cyclic because reduction cannot eliminate the whole hypergraph","Every possible database must be tested","No query corresponds to this structure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No variable has degree at most 1, and no edge is contained in another. The nonempty structure left by GYO reduction establishes that the hypergraph is cyclic."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 25 introduces conjunctive-query graphs and hypergraphs, useful structural classes, join trees, GYO reduction, and Yannakakis’s algorithm for acyclic queries.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–13"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=4","label":"PDF pages 4–13"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=14","label":"PDF pages 14–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=27","label":"PDF pages 27–36"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=28","label":"PDF pages 28–37"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–37","topics":["graph/hypergraph","treewidth context","join tree","running intersection","GYO reduction","acyclicity","Yannakakis","semijoin","Boolean evaluation"],"limitations":["The stated time bound does not include enumerating an arbitrarily large output. This lesson focuses on the decision problem."]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-26","lecture":26,"title":"Parallel query processing: bringing data to each operator","short":"Architectures · partitioning · distributed joins","group":"08 · Distributed systems","time":25,"lead":"Adding machines helps only when matching tuples reach the right place and no single machine does most of the work.","goals":["Compare shared-memory, shared-disk, and shared-nothing architectures.","Choose a partitioning strategy that fits the workload.","Estimate broadcast and shuffle costs, and identify bottlenecks."],"sections":[{"id":"l26-s1","title":"What is shared, and where does parallelism happen?","html":"<p>In a shared-memory architecture, processors share the same RAM address space. In a shared-disk architecture, each node has its own RAM but shares storage. In a shared-nothing architecture, each node has its own CPU, RAM, and disks, and nodes communicate over a network.</p><p>Inter-query parallelism runs independent queries concurrently. Within one query, inter-operator parallelism runs different operators at the same time, while intra-operator parallelism divides a scan or join into several pieces. For example, in a pipeline, a scan sends tuples to a filter before the scan has finished. Independent branches of a bushy query plan can also run at the same time.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 1–11</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"},{"id":"l26-s2","title":"Choosing a partitioning strategy","html":"<p>An application stores orders with customer_id, date, and region fields. If most requests retrieve one customer's orders, hash(customer_id) allows direct routing to the relevant partition. If most requests scan one month's orders, range(date) lets the system skip partitions outside that month. Round-robin partitioning balances the number of rows, but a key lookup usually has to contact several nodes.</p><p>If one customer accounts for 40% of the data, partitioning by hash(customer_id) can produce an uneven workload even with a good hash function. Distributing distinct keys evenly does not necessarily distribute tuples evenly. Replication creates additional copies to support availability and reads. Partitioning divides the data into separate pieces to increase capacity and enable parallel processing.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 12–19</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"},{"id":"l26-s3","title":"Parallel scans, sorts, and hash joins","html":"<p>Consider 800 GB of data and a read rate of 2 GB/s per machine. One machine needs 800/2 = 400 seconds. With an even split across four machines, each reads 800/4 = 200 GB and takes 200/2 = 100 seconds, excluding startup and network costs. For a range-based sort, machine 1 could hold keys &lt;100 and machine 2 keys ≥100. Sorting locally and then concatenating the results in range order produces a globally sorted result.</p><p>A parallel hash join must use the same partitioning function on the join keys of both inputs. A shuffle transfers data between nodes according to the partition key. For example, tuples with join key 42 in R and S must reach the same worker, where a local join can find the matching pairs. Sorting each hash partition locally and concatenating the outputs does not produce a global sort.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>For local joins to find every match, tuples with the same join key must meet at the same worker.</b> Using the same key name is not enough: both inputs need the same partitioning function, partition mapping, and placement of corresponding partitions.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 20–26</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"},{"id":"l26-s4","title":"Four join scenarios","html":"<ul><li>If the dimension table is already replicated, join each local fact partition with its local copy of the dimension table.</li><li>If both inputs are co-partitioned on the same join key with the same mapping, perform local joins.</li><li>If one input is small, broadcast that input to the workers.</li><li>If both inputs are large and partitioned on the wrong key, shuffle them by the join key.</li></ul><p>Consider R=120 GB, S=1 GB, and 8 workers. Broadcasting means sending a copy of S to the workers. If S already resides on one of the eight workers, this requires approximately 1×7 = 7 GB of network transfer. If tuples are initially distributed uniformly at random, shuffling both R and S is expected to move 7/8 of the total data to a different worker: (120+1)×7/8 = 105.875 GB. This idealized model excludes output and overhead. Broadcasting is suitable only if each worker can store and process S.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 27–42</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"},{"id":"l26-s5","title":"The critical path and data skew","html":"<p>Suppose four workers take 3, 3, 3, and 12 seconds. The parallel phase takes at least 12 seconds, rather than the average of (3+3+3+12)/4 = 5.25 seconds. Check for an overloaded partition before adding more machines.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>A parallel phase must wait for its slowest worker.</b> Skew concentrates work in one partition. Adding workers may not help if the heavy partition is still assigned to one worker.</p></aside><p>Predicate pushdown and early projection become even more valuable when they also reduce network traffic. State the metric explicitly: total bytes transferred, total CPU work, or wall-clock latency. A plan that reduces total work can still take longer if it concentrates too much work on one worker.</p><details><summary>Check your understanding: can two tables hashed on the same key always be joined locally?</summary><p>No. They also need the same partitioning function and mapping, with corresponding partitions placed together. Having the same key name is not enough.</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=43\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 43–45</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Workers take 4, 5, 5, and 15 seconds in one parallel phase. What is the lower bound on the phase duration?","options":["7.25 seconds","4 seconds","15 seconds","29 seconds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The phase must wait for its slowest worker. Coordination and network overhead may increase the total duration further."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 26 compares resource-sharing architectures, levels of parallelism, partitioning and replication, parallel scans, sorts, and joins, and cost models that account for network traffic and skew.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–11"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=12","label":"PDF pages 12–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pages 20–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=27","label":"PDF pages 27–42"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=43","label":"PDF pages 43–45"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–45","topics":["shared architectures","inter/intra-query parallelism","pipeline/bushy/partition parallelism","round-robin/hash/range","replication","parallel scan","parallel sort/hash join","broadcast/shuffle","network cost","skew"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"distributed"},{"id":"lecture-27","lecture":27,"title":"NoSQL: choosing a model for the system requirements","short":"Partitioning · replication · CAP · data models","group":"08 · Distributed systems","time":25,"lead":"A product name cannot answer the essential questions: how fresh must the data be, how will the application access it, and how should the system handle failures?","goals":["Distinguish sharding from replication.","Explain the trade-off during a network partition.","Compare key-value, document, column-family, and graph data models."],"sections":[{"id":"l27-s1","title":"Separate two design decisions","html":"<p>Sharding divides the data; replication creates copies of the data. A system can use both. A flexible schema does not mean the data has no structure. Structural checks may instead be performed by application code or configured validation rules.</p><p>NoSQL describes a family of approaches. The label does not imply that every NoSQL system lacks transactions or that every relational database is unable to scale. Examine a system's specific guarantees rather than relying on the SQL or NoSQL label.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 1–8</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"},{"id":"l27-s2","title":"A network partition and a stale read","html":"<p>Two regions, E and W, both store a price of 50. The connection between them fails, leaving no path for exchanging information. This is a <strong>network partition</strong>, even though the nodes themselves are still running. E accepts a write that changes the price to 60 and reports success. After that write completes, a user in W starts reading the price.</p><ul><li>If W returns the old price of 50, the system violates <strong>linearizability</strong>. The write completed before the read began, so the read must return 60, assuming there are no later writes.</li><li>If W waits for information from E, an indefinitely long partition may prevent the request from ever completing successfully. Rejecting the request also fails to provide availability in the CAP sense.</li></ul><p>In CAP, <strong>C</strong> means linearizability: operations appear to execute against a single copy and respect real-time ordering. <strong>A</strong> requires every request to a non-failing node eventually to receive a valid result. It does not merely mean responding quickly or returning an error code. During a partition, a system cannot guarantee both C and A for every possible execution.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>CAP concerns the guarantees a system can provide when communication is disrupted by a partition.</b> It is not a rule that lets a system freely choose any two of three properties at all times. CAP consistency also differs from ACID consistency, which concerns data invariants.</p></aside><p>In this example, neither E nor W has failed. The problem is that W lacks information about a write that has already succeeded. Waiting for communication to recover can preserve C, but cannot guarantee A if the partition never ends.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 9–11</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p><p class=\"source-note\">CAP clarification: <a href=\"https://groups.csail.mit.edu/tds/papers/Gilbert/Brewer6.pdf#page=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gilbert &amp; Lynch, original definitions and theorem</a>.</p>"},{"id":"l27-s3","title":"BASE and application requirements","html":"<p>Eventual consistency means that replicas eventually converge if no new updates occur and data exchange eventually succeeds. It does not promise that every read immediately after a write will see the new value.</p><p>Consider the application requirements: an article's view count can often lag by a few seconds, while selling the same last available seat to two customers is usually unacceptable. For a shopping cart, the system needs an explicit merge policy. If two locations each add a different item, should the merged cart retain both? The word “eventual” does not specify how conflicts are resolved.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p><b>Eventual consistency promises convergence under appropriate conditions.</b> By itself, it does not promise immediate visibility of the latest write, a bound on convergence time, or a particular rule for merging concurrent updates.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 11–14</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"},{"id":"l27-s4","title":"Four models and the questions they answer","html":"<ul><li><strong>Key-value:</strong> Retrieve a blob using a session ID. This works well when the exact key is known.</li><li><strong>Document:</strong> Store products with varying attributes and arrays, and filter on fields inside each document.</li><li><strong>Column-family:</strong> Organize many sparse fields around keys and access patterns. Do not confuse this model with columnar storage for OLAP.</li><li><strong>Graph:</strong> Represent people, accounts, and relationships, and query paths, connections, or patterns between them.</li></ul><p>Consider this JSON example: <code>{\"sku\":\"P7\",\"tags\":[\"blue\",\"small\"],\"spec\":{\"weight\":2}}</code>. Nested objects and arrays differ from a flat table, but they can be mapped to relations. The trade-offs involve access patterns, updates, and constraints.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 12–19</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"},{"id":"l27-s5","title":"Practice choosing a design","html":"<p>A shop has a frequently changing product catalog, a checkout process that requires consistent inventory, and a relationship-based recommendation system. These three workloads do not have to use the same data model. Write down their requirements separately: catalog lookups and filtering, atomic reservations, and graph traversal.</p><p>A good answer explains the cost of synchronizing the components rather than merely naming three products. The popularity chart in the PDF is a snapshot from March 2026. It is neither a performance benchmark nor a current recommendation. GQL, Cypher, and SPARQL represent graph-query language families; the choice should match the actual data model.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Source: <a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pages 18–21</a>. The examples and explanations on this learning page were written independently for this learning companion.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Two replicas cannot communicate, and one has acknowledged a new write. What is the problem if a later read at the other replica immediately returns the old value?","options":["It definitely violates durability.","It does not preserve linearizable consistency.","It loses the schema.","It cannot be a NoSQL system."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A read that starts after a successful write but returns the old value does not satisfy linearizability. This is the central trade-off illustrated by CAP."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 27 covers the motivation for NoSQL, sharding and replication, CAP and BASE, data models, and workload-driven choices. This learning material also explains how to interpret simplified CAP diagrams carefully.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–8"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=9","label":"PDF pages 9–11"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=11","label":"PDF pages 11–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=12","label":"PDF pages 12–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–21"},{"url":"https://groups.csail.mit.edu/tds/papers/Gilbert/Brewer6.pdf#page=2","label":"Gilbert & Lynch: CAP definitions and theorem, PDF pages 2–4"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–21","topics":["NoSQL motivation","sharding","replication","CAP","ACID versus CAP consistency","BASE","key-value","document/JSON","column-family","graph queries","workload choice"],"limitations":["The PDF simplifies the CAP diagram and its description of availability; this lesson provides a more precise interpretation.","The popularity chart is a historical snapshot from the PDF and is not presented as current data."]},"lab":"distributed"}];
window.GLOSSARY=[{"term":"Selection (σ)","definition":"Selection keeps the tuples that satisfy a condition.","lesson":"lecture-03"},{"term":"Projection (π)","definition":"Projection keeps selected attributes. Under set semantics, it also removes duplicate tuples.","lesson":"lecture-03"},{"term":"Buffer pool","definition":"The buffer pool is an area of memory divided into frames that hold database pages.","lesson":"lecture-05"},{"term":"Pin count","definition":"The pin count records active uses of a page. A pinned page cannot be selected for eviction.","lesson":"lecture-05"},{"term":"Slotted page","definition":"A slot directory maps each stable slot ID to the current location of its record within a page.","lesson":"lecture-06"},{"term":"B+ tree","definition":"A B+ tree is a balanced index whose leaves contain data entries and are linked to support range scans.","lesson":"lecture-09"},{"term":"Conflict serializability","definition":"A schedule is conflict-serializable if it is conflict-equivalent to a serial schedule. Its precedence graph must be acyclic.","lesson":"lecture-19"},{"term":"WAL","definition":"Write-ahead logging requires the relevant log records to reach durable storage before their changed data pages.","lesson":"lecture-21"},{"term":"Two-phase commit","definition":"This distributed commit protocol first gathers prepare votes and then announces a decision. It can block after a failure.","lesson":"lecture-22"},{"term":"EXCEPT ALL","definition":"For each tuple, bag difference returns max(mA − mB, 0) copies. SQLite does not support this operator; the Relational Lab simulates it separately.","lesson":"lecture-03"}];
